report:
The exact mixed component obtained from the Koszul formula is \[\operatorname{Ric}(T,W_1)=\frac{2kf}{(1+k^2)^2}
       \left(\frac{1}{a^3b^4}-a\right).
 \tag{R1}\] For completeness, in a unimodular invariant orthonormal frame one can compute this component as the sum of the bracket-norm, Killing-form and output-bracket terms. The first two cross terms cancel here. The pair \([X_2,X_3]\), including its reverse order, contributes \(-2kfa/(1+k^2)^2\); the pair \([W_2,W_3]\) contributes \(2kf/((1+k^2)^2a^3b^4)\). Their sum is (R1). The independently executed script instead forms all brackets, all Christoffel coefficients, the curvature tensor and its contraction, thereby checking the same identity without copying (74) or (75).

Here is the missing mechanism explicitly. Put \(q=h^2g\) and \(K=q'/2\). For tangential indices, \[\operatorname{Ric}^G_{ij}=\operatorname{Ric}^{q}_{ij}-K'_{ij}
       -(\operatorname{tr}_q K)K_{ij}+2K_{i\ell}q^{\ell m}K_{mj}.\] With \(\operatorname{tr}_g g'=0\) and cross-section dimension six this becomes \[\begin{aligned}
 \operatorname{Ric}^G_{ij}={}&\operatorname{Ric}^g_{ij}
 -(hh''+5(h')^2)g_{ij}-3hh'g'_{ij}\notag\\
 &-\frac{h^2}{2}g''_{ij}
   +\frac{h^2}{2}g'_{i\ell}g^{\ell m}g'_{mj}.
 \tag{R5}\end{aligned}\] The specialization \(h=1,g'=0\) already proves the discrepancy, without depending on the other coefficients in (R5).

