report:
For any nonconstant polynomial \(S\) and nearest complex root \(\alpha\) to \(\xi\), \[\left|\frac{S'(\xi)}{S(\xi)}\right|
 \le \sum_{\beta:S(\beta)=0}\frac1{|\xi-\beta|}
 \le \frac{\deg S}{|\xi-\alpha|}.\] This proves the root-distance estimate including repeated roots. Every root in the constructed basis has degree at most \(n\): added roots are integers or zero, and the others come from degree-at-most-\(n\) polynomials. The statement uses algebraic numbers without a real-root restriction; no unproved assertion that the nearest root is real is needed.

Source and location. Corollary 3(a)’s proof, pp. 28–29, source lines 1070–1097, uses the power saving \[s(n,k,\epsilon)=\frac{(n-1)(1-1/k)}
 {(n+2)/4-1/[n(n-1)]+\epsilon}.\] Its largest value for \(\epsilon\ge0\) is at zero. The displayed comparisons fail even at that value: \[s(28,5,0)=\frac{81648}{28345}<3,\qquad
 s(53,13,0)=\frac{66144}{18947}<\frac72.\] The second saving is also less than \(7/2\) at \(n=54,55\) and becomes greater at 56. A logarithmic factor only worsens the failed comparisons. The first term \(H^{n+1-k}\) being smaller does not remove the larger second term from their sum.

For \(\chi_{w,2v}\) (with the second index reduced modulo three), central phases cancel in the scalar product. Hence \[\begin{aligned}
 \langle\chi_{u,v}^2,\chi_{w,2v}\rangle
 &=C(q)+q^{-2}\sum_{\ell=0}^2
 (q\delta_{\ell u}-r')^2(q\delta_{\ell w}-r'),\\
 C(q)&=\frac{3D^3}{q^3r^2st}
       -\frac{3(r')^3}{q^3r}
       +\frac{t-3}{3t}
       =\frac{q^3-1}{9q^2}.\end{aligned}\] The remaining sum is \((2q^3+6q^2+1)/9\) if \(w=u\), and \((-q^3+1)/9\) otherwise. Therefore \[\boxed{\ \langle\chi_{u,v}^2,\chi_{w,2v}\rangle
       =\begin{cases}(q+2)/3,&w=u,\\0,&w\ne u.\end{cases}\ }\] Characters with the incompatible central character have scalar product zero. For odd \(q\equiv1\pmod3\), \((q+2)/3\) is odd. This proves the required uniqueness and parity in this family for all such \(q\), rather than extrapolating from examples. The script verifies the rational polynomial identity coefficient by coefficient and evaluates all nine scalar products for twelve values of \(q\), including \(q=13\) with degree \(672\) and multiplicity \(5\). One even \(q\) is included only as an algebraic diagnostic, not as an odd-characteristic case.

For Lemma 5.2, the first inequality follows from the entropy-increment inequality of Lemma A.1, and the second from conditioning reducing entropy: \[\begin{aligned}
 \mathrm d[X;Y-Z]-\mathrm d[X;Y]&\le\tfrac12(\mathrm H[Y-Z]-\mathrm H[Y]),\\
 \mathrm d[X;Y\mid Y-Z]-\mathrm d[X;Y]&\le\tfrac12(\mathrm H[Y-Z]-\mathrm H[Z]).\end{aligned}\] Here \(Y,Z\) are independent and \(X\) may be replaced by an independent copy. These formulas reproduce the signs in the bound v2 text. The four reference-distance penalties in the first estimate have cancelling \(\tfrac14(\mathrm H[X_1]-\mathrm H[X_2])\) terms. Thus (3.13) indeed gives \(I_1\le2\eta k\). The second estimate, with \(D=\mathrm d[X_1;X_1]+\mathrm d[X_2;X_2]\), gives \[D\le2k+\frac{2(2\eta k-I_1)}{1-\eta}.\] The denominator is positive. Exchanging \(X_1\) and its independent identically distributed copy preserves the total sum and proves \(I_2=I_3\); equality is not inferred merely from similar notation.

