The inspected core chain is \[\begin{aligned}
 &\text{geometric Sen injectivity}
 \Longrightarrow \text{full Sen injectivity}\\
 &\quad\Longrightarrow \text{maximal invariant dimension}
 \Longrightarrow \delta=0\\
 &\quad\Longrightarrow \text{pointwise pre-perfectoidness}.
 \end{aligned}\] Boundary root divisibility is then needed for the open variety; the almost comparison and a dimension bound imply integral torsion-cohomology vanishing. The Shimura application additionally imports the locally split injectivity theorem quoted as Theorem 11.2. The checks below distinguish deductions verified here from results used conditionally.

Write \(\Delta(t)=\det\Phi(t)=(1-\sigma)(4-t^2)\). At nonsingular \(s,t\), the displayed matrix is \[M=\Phi(s)^{-1}\Phi(t)\frac{\Delta(s)}{\Delta(t)}.\] Since a scalar multiplies a two-by-two matrix, \[\det M=\frac{\Delta(t)}{\Delta(s)}
 \left(\frac{\Delta(s)}{\Delta(t)}\right)^2
 =\frac{s^2-4}{t^2-4},\] generally not one. For \(p=101\), \(\sigma=2\), \(s=3\), \(t=4\), both matrices are invertible and the determinant is \(5/12=93\) in \(\mathbf F_{101}\). All conditions of the displayed calculation hold. Scalars may be ignored projectively, but not in a claim of membership in \(\mathrm{SL}_2\). A consistent projective argument or a square-root normalization with its field conditions is needed.

Set \(s=\sigma t+4(1-\sigma)/t\). Direct multiplication gives, away from singular parameters, \[\frac{\operatorname{adj}(\Phi(s))\Phi(t)}
 {(1-\sigma)(4/t-t)}
 =
 \begin{pmatrix}
 \sigma(1+\sigma)t+4\sigma(1-\sigma)/t&
 -\sigma^2t^2+4(1-\sigma)^2\\
 1&0
 \end{pmatrix}.\] Equation (72) instead has \(4(1-\sigma)/t\) in the upper-left entry. Printed minus calculated is \(4(1-\sigma)^2/t\), nonzero for \(p>2\), \(\sigma\ne1\), \(t\ne0\). This cannot be absorbed into a projective scalar because the lower-left entries are both one. Thus the subsequent specialized \(g_\pm\) have not been obtained from the actual \(\Phi\) by the stated calculation. The symbolic multiplication is saved in the evidence.

