 \frac{a}{(\ell+r)^2}
 \leq C\{\lambda^2+e^{-\ell/C}+e^{-r/C}\}.\] It does not prove the claimed uniform assertion for all sufficiently large \(\ell\) and all \(r\geq r_0\), with fixed \(r_0\) and constants independent of \(\ell\). With \(r\) fixed and \(\ell\to\infty\), \((\ell+r)^2 e^{-r/C}\) is unbounded. With \(\ell\) fixed and \(r\to\infty\), \((\ell+r)^2 e^{-\ell/C}\) is unbounded. This remains a problem even after restricting to \(r\geq\ell\). For example, with all displayed constants set to one, \(\ell=100\), \(r=10^{60}\) and \(\lambda=0\), inequality [eq:weak] (#eq:weak) is compatible with zero: its left side is about \(10^{-120}\) and its right side exceeds \(e^{-100}\). For arbitrary fixed constants, sufficiently disparate parameters give the same non-implication.

