Fix any \(\rho>0\), put \(R=e^{2\pi\rho}>1\), and choose an integer \(N\ge1\) such that \(R^{-2N}\le1/4\). For \(\theta\in[0,2\pi)\) and \(|\operatorname{Im}z|<\rho\), define \[\begin{aligned}
 u(z)&=R^{-1}e^{2\pi iz},\\
 H_\theta(z)&=u(z)^N
 \exp\left(-\frac{e^{i\theta}+u(z)}{e^{i\theta}-u(z)}\right),\\
 h_\theta(z)&=\frac{H_\theta(z)+\overline{H_\theta(\bar z)}}{16}.\end{aligned}\] Here \(|u|<1\), and \[\operatorname{Re}\frac{e^{i\theta}+u}{e^{i\theta}-u}
 =\frac{1-|u|^2}{|e^{i\theta}-u|^2}>0.\] Consequently \(H_\theta\) is holomorphic, one-periodic and bounded by \(1\). The reflected term is holomorphic as well. The function \(h_\theta\) is real-valued on the real axis, is one-periodic, and satisfies \(\|h_\theta\|_\rho\le1/8\).

For \(M=3^k\), define the 1-bounded cylinder function \[f_M(x)=\exp\left(\frac{2\pi i(x\bmod M)^2}{M}\right).\] Using the order-two cube formula (21) on p. 18, its second derivative is \(\exp(2\pi i\,2h_1h_2/M)\), independent of \(x\). Hence \[\lVert f_M\rVert_{2,\mathrm{HK}}^4
 =\frac1{M^2}\sum_{h_1,h_2\bmod M}e^{2\pi i\,2h_1h_2/M}
 =\frac1M.\] For each fixed \(h_1\), the inner geometric sum is zero unless \(M\mid2h_1\). Since \(M\) is odd, only \(h_1=0\) contributes, with sum \(M\). This proves the identity for all \(k\), without a numerical approximation.

The statement on p. 28 indexes \(a\) by positive multi-indices, but uses \(a_{h-h'}\) and then an average over \([H]^s\). Differences include zero and negative coordinates. For a sequence extended to \(\mathbb Z^s\), the exact identity is \[\mathbb E_{h,h'\in[H]^s}a_{h-h'}
 =H^{-s}\!\!\sum_{\substack{d\in\mathbb Z^s\\|d_i|<H\ \forall i}}
 \prod_{i=1}^s(1-|d_i|/H)\,a_d .\] The printed positive-box identity and inequality fail, for example, for \(s=1\), \(a_0=1\), and \(a_d=0\) otherwise: the left side is \(1/H\), the printed right side zero. For nonnegative \(a\), the safe bound is the same sum over the full difference box without its weights. Any replacement by a positive-box average requires explicit symmetry and boundary terms. Those application-level adjustments were not independently completed in this review; no further main-theorem counterexample is claimed from this local error.

Equation reference: eq:local. Write \(U=U_{y,t}\), \(\Phi=\phi_t+\lambda\psi_t\), and \(dV=i^{n^2}\Omega_t\wedge\overline{\Omega_t}\). The preceding local bound is \(\Phi\leq\lambda u(y)+C\lambda\varepsilon\) on \(U\). For the nonnegative weighted integrand, positivity and restriction to \(U\) give \[\begin{aligned}
 I&=\int_{X_t}|s|_{h^{\otimes l}}^2e^{-2l\Phi}\,dV
       \geq\int_U |s|_{h^{\otimes l}}^2e^{-2l\Phi}\,dV \notag\\
  &\geq e^{-2l\lambda u(y)-Cl\lambda\varepsilon}
          \int_U |s|_{h^{\otimes l}}^2\,dV .\end{aligned}\] The harmless change in \(C\) absorbs the factor two. Taking logarithms and dividing by \(2l\) yields the annular norm bound actually used in the next line of the paper. The saved late audit tracks its combination with the Ohsawa–Takegoshi upper bound and the no-cancellation estimate.

Equation reference: eq:dyadic. The endpoint operator bounds used in Proposition 2.4 are \[\left\lVert W_N\right\rVert_{2\to2}\lesssim T^{-1}N,\qquad
 \left\lVert W_N\right\rVert_{1\to\infty}\lesssim
 T^{-1}\lambda^{(n-1)/2}N^{1-\alpha},\] on the indicated product of counting and Riemannian measure spaces, with dyadic \(2\le N\le T\) (printed pp. 969–970). Interpolating with weight \(\vartheta=1-2/q\) on the second bound gives \[
 \left\lVert W_N\right\rVert_{q'\to q}
 \lesssim T^{-1}\lambda^{2\mu(q)}
 N^{\,1-\alpha(1-2/q)}.\] Thus the sum is uniformly bounded in \(T\), after the factor \(T^{-1}\), when \(\alpha>q/(q-2)\). Taking the square root in the \(UU^*\) estimate yields \(\lambda^{\mu(q)}T^{-1/2}\), exactly (2.52). At \(q=q_c\), the exponent of \(N\) is \(1-2\alpha/(n+1)\). Summing the geometric series gives \[\left\lVert U\right\rVert_{2\to q_c}\lesssim\lambda^{1/q_c}
 \begin{cases}
 T^{-\alpha/(n+1)},&\alpha<(n+1)/2,\\
 T^{-1/2},&\alpha>(n+1)/2.
 \end{cases}\] The nonpositive-curvature value \(\alpha=(n-1)/2\) therefore gives \(T^{-1/q_c}\). The arbitrary fixed decay order in the strictly negative case allows \(\alpha>q/(q-2)\) for each fixed \(q>2\). These are precisely the different gains claimed.

An explicit model independently verifies the flat scaling. On \(\mathbb T^n=(\mathbb R/2\pi\mathbb Z)^n\) with normalized Haar measure, define \[F_{k,m}(x)=(2m+1)^{-(n-1)/2}e^{ikx_1}
       \prod_{j=2}^{n}\sum_{r=-m}^{m}e^{irx_j}.\] Its \(L^2\) norm is exactly one. Every frequency satisfies \[0\le\sqrt{k^2+|r'|^2}-k
 \le\frac{(n-1)m^2}{2k}.\] Choose \(m=\lfloor\sqrt{k\delta/(n-1)}\rfloor\) with \(k\delta\to\infty\). The spectrum then lies in \([k,k+\delta]\). On the transverse box \(|x_j|\le c/m\), with fixed sufficiently small \(c>0\), all summands have uniformly positive real parts. Hence \[\left\lVert F_{k,m}\right\rVert_q\gtrsim
 m^{(n-1)(1/2-1/q)}
 \asymp(k\delta)^{\mu(q)}.\] For \(n=2\), \(k=256\), \(\delta=1/4\), and \(m=8\), exact finite arithmetic gives \(\left\lVert F\right\rVert_{4}^{4}=193/17\), while the largest frequency increment is at most \(1/8\). This finite check is illustrative; the preceding analytic argument is the asymptotic justification. Neither calculation replaces the paper’s construction for arbitrary compact flat quotients.

For clarity, an explicit global small nondegenerate condition for this type is \[\theta(v)=-\frac2{23}(2g(v)-2+n(v))
 +\frac{43}{230}\mathbf1_{\{1\text{ at }v\}}
 -\frac3{230}\mathbf1_{\{2\text{ at }v\}}.\] The total is zero and the terms are additive under contraction. Its values on the two-vertex cycle are \(1/10,-1/10\); on a separating rational tail carrying both markings they are \(2/23,-2/23\). These magnitudes are below half the relevant cut valence, so multidegree zero is stable. Every cut containing a proper nonempty selection of original vertices has a nonintegral stability endpoint. Exceptional-vertex cuts have exactly the equality exceptions permitted by Definition 30. The stable graphs of type \((1,2)\) and their contractions exhaust these cases, giving nondegeneracy here.

Equation reference: eq:factorial, eq:nonzero. Normalize \(v_p(p)=1\) and let \(p\) be odd. Choose \(\pi\in K\) with \(v_p(\pi)=\alpha=1/(p-1)\). The notation in [eq:tate] (#eq:tate) means, in one variable, \[A=R\langle\pi^{-1}\partial\rangle=R\langle Y\rangle,\qquad \partial=\pi Y.\] Membership in a Tate algebra requires that the coefficients in \(Y\) tend to zero. The purported exponential has coefficients \(a_n=\pi^n/n!\). For \(n=p^k\), the elementary factorial valuation gives \[
 v_p((p^k)!)=\sum_{j=1}^{k}p^{k-j}=\frac{p^k-1}{p-1}.\] Consequently, \[
 v_p(a_{p^k})=\frac{p^k}{p-1}-\frac{p^k-1}{p-1}
 =\frac1{p-1}
 \quad\text{for every }k\geq0.\] This subsequence has fixed nonzero norm, so \(a_n\) does not tend to zero. Therefore \[\exp(\partial)\notin R\langle\pi^{-1}\partial\rangle .\] The proposed cocycle is not defined in the stated algebra, and the stated inverse of [eq:phi] (#eq:phi) is not an element of that algebra. This is an infinite-subsequence proof, not an extrapolation from finite computation.

