The normalized Chern-class formula, Markman’s (7.11) [Markman], gives \[\frac{a_{S^{[n]}}}{\operatorname{rk}U^{[n]}}
       =\frac{\epsilon H}{4gtd^2}-\frac\delta2 .\] Using \(\rho(H-2gtd^2\delta)=\epsilon(D+4gtdB)\), both orientation signs give \[\rho\left(\frac{\epsilon H}{4gtd^2}-\frac\delta2\right)
   =\frac Bd+\frac{D}{4gtd^2}
      +\frac{\epsilon-1}{2}\rho(\delta).\] The second summand is in \(\operatorname{Pic}(X)_\mathbb Q\) and the last is integral for both \(\epsilon=1\) and \(\epsilon=-1\). The induced target twist is therefore \([-B/d]=\alpha\). For a product kernel, the convention is source twist with a minus sign and target twist with a plus sign in its twisting class. The minus sign in the paper’s definition \(\alpha_E=-[c_1(E)/\operatorname{rk}E]\) is consistent with the displayed source/target equivalence. No factor of two remains unaccounted for.

For Lemma 5.6, pages 24–26, write \(m_j=|\mathcal A_{(j)}|\) and \(C_j=\mathcal C(\mathcal A_{(j)})\). The recurrence has the form \[C_j\ge C_{j-1}\sqrt{\frac{m_{j-1}}{m_j}}-E,
 \qquad E=O((\log Y)^{-B/2}).\] Multiplying by \(\sqrt{m_j}\), summing and using \(m_j\le m_0\) gives \[C_j\ge C_0\sqrt{\frac{m_0}{m_j}}
          -jE\sqrt{\frac{m_0}{m_j}}.\] This is the mass-weighted error in the manuscript, not an unjustified loss independent of the retained mass. With \(j=O(\log Y)\) and \(B>2\) fixed sufficiently large, \(jE=o(1)\). The mixing upper bound on \(C_j\), together with \(C_0>0\), then enforces a uniform positive lower bound on \(m_j/m_0\). The proper-subset argument terminates because the configurations are finite. The bounded number of stronger expansion stages in Proposition 5.8 is kept separate from the many nearly lossless stages.

Over \(\mathbb C_3\), the two distinct roots give \[Y_{\mathbb C_3}
   =\{\sqrt2\}\times\mathbb P^1_{\mathbb C_3}
     \ \amalg\
     \{-\sqrt2\}\times\mathbb P^1_{\mathbb C_3}.\] In local coordinates the two defining linear factors have nonzero derivative; there is no nilpotent or singular intersection hidden in the example. The two components are disjoint and open and closed. Thus \(Y\) is not geometrically connected, despite being integral over \(K\) and of positive dimension.

