Theorem 1.6 assumes a flat finite-type map from a regular scheme to a strictly henselian smooth trait over algebraically closed \(k\), an action over the trait, an isolated fixed-point locus, and smoothness away from that point. It asserts, only for \(\sigma\ne1\), \[\operatorname{Tr}\!\left(\sigma;
 \Psi_{\mathrm{pr}'}\mathcal L(ft')_{(x,\infty')}\right)
       =-a_{X,G}(\sigma).\] The connected alteration of the parameter line identifies the two specializations of the relative trace. At zero the trace is the diagonal–graph intersection length; at infinity the nearby cycles are supported at the isolated point. Proposition 2.5 identifies their virtual class with minus the total-dimension class of vanishing cycles. Section 2.6 then invokes the Kato–Saito–Saito reduction, paper reference [13], Lemma 5.3.

More generally, for \(t=g(v)\), \(v=q(u)\), the chain rule gives \[\operatorname{ord}_u\frac{dt}{du}
 =\operatorname{ord}_u\frac{dv}{du}
   + e(q)\operatorname{ord}_v\frac{dt}{dv}.\] The valuation factor comes from substitution: a uniformizer \(v\) has \(u\)-valuation \(e(q)\). For the finite local quotient under the stated residue-field conditions, \(e(q)=|G|\). Thus the correctly normalized scalar statement is \[\mu_f-a_{X,G}(1)=|G|\,\operatorname{disc}(g).\]

