Equation reference: eq:correct. Set \(T=\sqrt y\), \(\epsilon_k=(-1)^{k/2-1}\), and \[F(T)=\sum_{d,s\ge1}\frac{Q(d)\sqrt d}{s^{3/2}}
             \cos\left(\frac{4\pi sT}{d}-\frac{3\pi}{4}\right).\] The standard fixed-order expansion [dlmf] gives \[J_{k-1}(z)=\epsilon_k\sqrt{\frac2{\pi z}}\,
              \cos(z-3\pi/4)+O_k(z^{-3/2}),\qquad z\to+\infty.\] The phase follows by subtracting \((k-1)\pi/2+\pi/4\). Substitution into one term of [eq:exact] (#eq:exact) gives \[\alpha T\,\frac{Q(d)}s\sqrt{\frac2{\pi(4\pi sT/d)}}
 =\frac{\alpha}{\pi\sqrt2}\,T^{1/2}
                 \frac{Q(d)\sqrt d}{s^{3/2}}.\] Consequently the corrected formula is \[
 \boxed{\mathcal M_k(y)=
   \frac{\alpha(-1)^{k/2-1}}{\pi\sqrt2}\,
         y^{1/4}F(\sqrt y)+O_k(1).}\]

For \(T\ge1\), split the sum into \(d\le sT\) and \(d>sT\). In the first range the Bessel argument is at least \(4\pi\), and the summed remainder is \[\ll_k T^{-1/2}\sum_{s\ge1}s^{-5/2}
                      \sum_{d\le sT}d^{-1/2}
 \ll_k\sum_{s\ge1}s^{-2}\ll_k1.\] In the second range, boundedness of \(J_{k-1}\) on \([0,4\pi]\) bounds the exact contribution by \[\ll_k T\sum_{s\ge1}s^{-1}\sum_{d>sT}d^{-2}\ll_k1.\] The corresponding proposed leading terms are bounded by \[\ll T^{1/2}\sum_{s\ge1}s^{-3/2}\sum_{d>sT}d^{-3/2}\ll1.\] This proves the uniform \(O_k(1)\) remainder in [eq:correct] (#eq:correct). It avoids the small-argument failure of the printed expression \(O(\min\{z^{-3/2},1\})\): the leading cosine term diverges at \(z=0\), whereas \(J_{k-1}(z)\) does not.

Equation reference: eq:abel. Let \(A(x)=\sum_{d\le x}Q(d)\). The asserted estimate would give \(A(x)=L(0)-\Delta/x+R(x)\). For \(\Re s>0\), partial summation then implies \[
 L(s)=L(0)-\frac{s\Delta}{s+1}
             +s\int_1^\infty R(x)x^{-s-1}\,dx.\] Fix \(0<\varepsilon<1/4\). If \(R(x)=O(x^{-2+\varepsilon})\), the integral is holomorphic on \(\Re s>-2+\varepsilon\). Formula [eq:abel] (#eq:abel) would continue \(L\) into that half-plane with no possible pole except \(s=-1\). But \(s_0\) belongs to that half-plane and is a nonremovable pole, contradicting uniqueness of meromorphic continuation.

At PDF pp. 13–14 (printed pp. 639–640), the error is bounded, up to arbitrarily small powers, by \[\frac{Y}{P}+\frac{Y}{\sqrt T}
       +X^{1/2}T^{3/16}+\frac{Y^2}{X}
       +\frac{Y(PX)^{1/2}}{T}.\] The text chooses \(T=Y^{5/6}P^{5/12}X^{-1/12}\) and says the third and last terms are equal. In fact equality requires \[T^{19/16}=YP^{1/2},\qquad \boxed{T=Y^{16/19}P^{8/19}.}\] The defect reaches the printed derivation within Theorem 1’s own parameter range. Set \[\delta_1=\frac1{100},\qquad \delta_2=\frac14,\qquad
 X=P\to\infty\ \text{through primes},\qquad Y=X^{3/4}.\] These satisfy the conditions on PDF pp. 1–2: \(0<\delta_1<1/11\), \(2\delta_1<\delta_2<(4-18\delta_1)/13\), and \(P\ll X^{1+\delta_1}\). The printed cutoff is \(T=X^{23/24}\). After the prescribed normalization \(Y^{-1}\sqrt{P/X}\), the last term of (7) becomes \(P/T=X^{1/24}\); it does not even provide a decaying error. Section 3.4 (PDF pp. 38–39) explicitly substitutes Proposition 3.1 into the proof of Theorem 1 and asserts a positive power saving throughout that parameter range. The corrected cutoff is \(T=X^{20/19}\), for which the third and last normalized terms both equal \(X^{-1/19}\). This comparison concerns the available upper bound, not a lower bound for the actual class-number error or a counterexample to the theorem.

