For the nondefocusing interpolation on p. 78, write \(F=|u_\lambda|^2\). The one-dimensional inequality \[\|F\|_{L^3_x}\lesssim
 \|F\|_{L^1_x}^{5/9}\|\partial_xF\|_{L^2_x}^{4/9}\] gives, from the energy and diagonal bilinear bounds, \[\|u_\lambda\|_{L^9_tL^6_x}
 \lesssim C\epsilon c_\lambda\lambda^{1/9-s},\qquad
 \|u_\lambda\|_{L^6_{t,x}}
 \lesssim T^{1/18}C\epsilon c_\lambda\lambda^{1/9-s}.\] On an interval \(T=\eta\epsilon^{-6}\), the ratio of the sixth power to the target \(\epsilon^4c_\lambda^4\lambda^{-4s-1}\) is at most \[\eta^{1/3}C^6c_\lambda^2\lambda^{5/3-2s}.\] For \(s>1\) the frequency exponent is negative, and choosing the interval constant sufficiently small closes this part of the bootstrap. This checks the time and frequency exponents independently, conditional on the bilinear input.

Counterevidence and repair of the required valuation step. Unramifiedness of the special-point field is not needed for the infinite-order argument. Retain an arbitrary uniformizer \(\varpi\) of \(L=E(sK_0)_v\), let \(e=e(L/\mathbb Q_p)\), and normalize \(v_p(p)=1\). Since \(\mu_x\) is defined over \(L\), \[v_p\!\left(\chi\!\left(N_{L/\mathbb Q_p}(\mu_x(\varpi))\right)\right)
 =\frac{1}{e}\sum_{\sigma:L\hookrightarrow\overline{\mathbb Q}_p}
       \langle\chi,{}^\sigma\mu_x\rangle.\] All conjugate cocharacters are dominant for the rational Borel. On each nontrivial adjoint factor there is a positive root with positive pairing for at least one summand, and all summands for that root are nonnegative. Thus the displayed valuation is positive. The compact right side of Equation (7) has zero character valuations, so the projection of \(q\) has infinite order. Equation (6) transfers this to the Frobenius element. This reasoning uses compactness, not maximality of that compact subgroup.

The displayed equality can therefore be replaced by an inequality with exactly the upper bound printed afterward. This is decisive counterevidence to treating the missing tail as a failure of the perimeter theorem. In particular, \[\frac{\mathbb E|\partial\mathrm{Vor}_\lambda(S)|}{V}
 \leq\frac{\delta}{2}+\frac2\pi(1+\delta)^3
 \leq\frac2\pi(1+\delta)^4\] for sufficiently small \(\lambda\) depending only on \(\delta\). The surface-dependent \(o_S(\varepsilon)\) has already vanished. No interchange of a uniform moduli-space supremum with that error limit is necessary.

For the even, odd, and Suzuki characters, the restrictions to the Borel have squared norms \[\frac{(q-1)^2+(q-1)}{q(q-1)}=1,\quad
 \frac{(q-1)^2/4+(q-1)(q+1)/4}{q(q-1)/2}=1,\] \[\frac{(q-1)^2(2q)/4+(q-1)(2q)/4+q(q-1)(2q)/4}
 {q^2(q-1)}=1.\] The respective positive centralizer-dimension gaps are \(q/2-1\), \((q-3)/4\), and \(q(q-2)/4\) on the allowed parameter ranges. The non-Suzuki block calculations were checked against the displayed matrices. The Suzuki correction is given in Problem 1.

Set \(m=(q-1)/2\) and \[a=\frac{-1+\sqrt q}{2},\qquad b=\frac{-1-\sqrt q}{2}.\] For the character listed in Proposition 4.1 (pp. 3–4), \(a^2+b^2=(q+1)/2\). Using precisely the class counts stated there gives \[\begin{aligned}
 \langle\chi|_B,\chi|_B\rangle_B
 &=\frac{2m^2+(q-1)(a^2+b^2)}{q(q-1)}=1,\\
 \langle\chi|_{C_4},\chi|_{C_4}\rangle_{C_4}
 &=\frac{2m^2}{4}=\frac{(q-1)^2}{8},\\
 \langle\chi|_{2D_{q+1}},\chi|_{2D_{q+1}}\rangle_{2D_{q+1}}
 &=\frac{2m^2+(q-1)}{2(q+1)}=\frac{q-1}{4}.\end{aligned}\] The \(C_4\) noncentral elements have character zero. The \(q+1\) order-four elements in the binary dihedral subgroup also contribute zero, while the remaining paired noncentral cyclic elements have character of absolute value one. For a unitary representation, the squared character norm is the sum of squared irreducible multiplicities, which is the real dimension of its unitary centralizer. The calculations therefore give all three conclusions of Proposition 4.2. They check the use of the supplied character table; they do not independently construct Dornhoff’s character table.

In Proposition 7.1 (pp. 5–6), the three-factor product map is invariant under the displayed action: substituting the three transformed inverse factors cancels the \(\alpha_{v_1}\) and \(\alpha_{v_3}\) factors consecutively. The action is free, since the first and last fixed-coordinate equations successively force both acting elements to be the identity. Compactness makes it proper. The excess of the quotient dimension over the target dimension is \[\frac{q-1}{4}-\frac{(q-1)^2}{8}
 =\frac{(q-1)(3-q)}8<0
 \quad(q\equiv13\pmod {24}).\] A smooth map from this lower-dimensional compact manifold cannot be surjective, giving degree zero for the original map with the same image.

For the enlarged action in Proposition 7.2, the fixed first, second and third coordinates force \(\alpha_1=\alpha_K=\alpha_3=I\), respectively. Also, \[(\alpha_1^{-1}\tau_0)^{-1}
 (\alpha_3^{-1}\tau_2\alpha_1)^{-1}
 (\alpha_K^{-1}\tau_3\alpha_3)^{-1}
 (\tau_1\alpha_K)^{-1}
 =\tau_0^{-1}\tau_2^{-1}\tau_3^{-1}\tau_1^{-1}.\] This verifies the quotient factorization without assuming commutativity of the factors. The dimension excess is bounded above by \[\frac{q-1}{4}-\frac{(q-1)^2}{96}
 =\frac{(q-1)(25-q)}{96}<0.\] Indeed, \(q\equiv5\pmod {24}\) and \(q>5\) imply \(q\geq29\). The excluded value \(q=5\) is not a counterexample to the stated proposition.

First fix \(h_0\in(h_*,a]\). The input triple has third parameter \(h_0\), input center \(u_{h_0}\), and fixed finite radius \(r_{h_0}\). Let \(h_n\downarrow h_*\) with \(h_n<h_0\). Define an increasing piecewise linear circle homeomorphism \(\phi_n\) which fixes the east and west line endpoints, sends \(\pi/2\pm\theta_{h_0}\) to \(\pi/2\pm\theta_{h_n}\), is linear on the three intervening northern intervals, and is the identity on the remaining circle. Its nonunit slopes are \[m_n=\frac{\pi/2-\theta_{h_n}-\theta_a}
           {\pi/2-\theta_{h_0}-\theta_a},\qquad
 M_n=\frac{\theta_{h_n}}{\theta_{h_0}}.\] They obey fixed positive bounds \[0<m_*=\frac{\pi/2-2\theta_a-\theta_a}
                 {\pi/2-\theta_{h_0}-\theta_a}
 \le m_n\le1,\qquad
 1\le M_n\le M_*=\frac{2\theta_a}{\theta_{h_0}}.\] The radial extension \[F_n(re^{i\theta})=re^{i\phi_n(\theta)},\qquad F_n(0)=0,\] has radial and angular length factors \(1\) and \(\phi_n'\). The hyperbolic conformal factor is unchanged because the radius is unchanged. Integrating the length bounds across the finitely many break rays, and applying them also to the inverse, proves a global bi-Lipschitz bound \(\max(M_*,m_*^{-1})\), including at the origin.

The sharp ratio is stronger than what the ensuing coarse estimates need. The actual published Lemma 6.6 of Calderon–Farre, inspected in the geometry check, supplies for spine weight \(c\) and dual length \(a\) \[\min\!\left\{\log3,\,
 2\operatorname{artanh}
 \frac{\tanh(\log\sqrt3)}{\cosh(c/2)}\right\}
 \le a\le\frac{2\pi}{c}.\] In particular, for small \(a\), \[c\ge2\operatorname{arcosh}
       \left(\frac{1/2}{\tanh(a/2)}\right)
     =2\log(1/a)+O(1).\] This is enough for the logarithmic lower bound required in Section 3. For Lemma 4.1, replacing the incorrect exponential comparison by \[2\delta(\varepsilon)+\frac{2\pi e^\varepsilon}{K_1}
       <\log\sqrt3\] provides the needed uniform short-arc threshold by first choosing \(\varepsilon\) small and then \(K_1\) large. These inequalities, rather than a globally asserted ratio two, are the verified substitute.

For Proposition 6.3, inversion and division of the geometric comparison, using \(\ell_X\ge\sqrt L\), \(0<\varepsilon<1\), and \(e^\varepsilon<3\), give exactly \[e^{-2\varepsilon}\frac{w_Y}{\ell_Y}-\frac{3K}{\sqrt L}
 \le\frac{w_X}{\ell_X}\le
 e^{2\varepsilon}\frac{w_Y}{\ell_Y}+\frac{3K}{\sqrt L}.\] This verifies (6.8)–(6.11) under the stated geometric hypotheses. The shrinking Morse bound was checked in its actual application \(\mathbb H^2\), with a parametrized compactness argument and the inverse-map step for two-sided Hausdorff distance.

