report:
Multiplication of its Torelli pullback by \(\lambda_{g-2}\) eliminates every extremal tree except the single edge between the genus-one root and a leaf of genus \(g-1\). With at least three positive-genus vertices, the largest nonzero Hodge index is at most \(g-3\); with just two such vertices there can be no stable genus-zero vertex in an unmarked tree. The required integrals are \[J_h=\frac{|B_{2h}|}{2h(2h)!},\qquad
 I_g=\frac{|B_{2g}||B_{2g-2}|}
 {2(2g-2)!(2g)(2g-2)}\quad(g\ge2).\] The ratio is exactly \[\frac{J_{g-1}/24}{I_g}
                       =\frac{g}{6|B_{2g}|}.\] For \(g=2\) it is \(10\), consistent with the degree-two ordered product map onto the reduced decomposable divisor.

Let \(j:\mathcal A_k\times\mathcal A_{g-k}\to\mathcal A_g\). Writing \(p_1,p_2\) for the projections, the formulas are \[\begin{aligned}
 N_j&=p_1^*\mathbb E_k^\vee\otimes p_2^*\mathbb E_{g-k}^\vee,\\
 \operatorname{Obs}
   &=p_1^*(\bigwedge^2\mathbb E_k^\vee)\oplus
                              p_2^*(\bigwedge^2\mathbb E_{g-k}^\vee).\end{aligned}\] The ranks satisfy \(k(g-k)+\binom{k}{2}+\binom{g-k}{2}=\binom g2\). The staircase Schur-determinant induction in Lemma 41 uses \(\lambda_g=0\) and \(\lambda_{g-1}^2=0\) at the indicated steps. Dualization gives the signs \((-1)^{\binom{k}{2}}\) and \((-1)^{\binom{g-k}{2}}\) in Proposition 14. This direct calculation survives the general-locus definition correction. Speculation 15 remains a speculation.

The parameter norm is \[\left\lVert h\right\rVert_s^{\operatorname{Lip}(\lambda)}
  =\sup_{\xi\in O}\left\lVert h(\xi)\right\rVert_{H^s}
   +\lambda\sup_{\xi\ne\eta}
       \frac{\left\lVert h(\xi)-h(\eta)\right\rVert_{H^{s-1}}}{|\xi-\eta|}.\] A scalar weighted Lipschitz bound therefore controls the ordinary Lipschitz constant only after division by \(\lambda\). This distinction matters in the measure estimate.

Lemma 3.3 supplies \(r_\varepsilon=g+O(\varepsilon)\) and claims \(\mathcal G(\varepsilon r_\varepsilon)=O(\varepsilon^5)\). The rescaled equation is \[\mathcal F(\rho)=\varepsilon^{-2-\mu}
       \mathcal G(\varepsilon r_\varepsilon+\varepsilon^{1+\mu}\rho)=0,\quad 0<\mu<1.\] Its derivative has a leading transport part, \(-\mathcal H/2\), and an order-\(\varepsilon^2\) operator supported on spatial modes \(\pm1\). Propositions 4.1–4.3 straighten the transport and identify that finite-rank operator; Proposition 5.1 inverts its degenerate block; Propositions 5.4–5.5 perturb and conjugate the approximate inverse. Section 6 then uses \[N_n=N_0^{(3/2)^n},\quad N_0=\varepsilon^{-\delta},\quad
 \lambda=\varepsilon^{2+\delta},\quad b_0=3-\mu .\] Thus a mistaken order-\(\varepsilon^2\) coefficient, an insufficient initial residual, or a missing small-norm input lies on a necessary link of the stated construction.

There is a direct smooth, real, zero-mean test. Take \(b=t\sin(2\theta)\), \(|t|<1/2\), so that \(f'>0\). Then \(\mathcal B_t=\operatorname{Id}+tD+O(t^2)\), with \(Dh=\partial_\theta(\sin(2\theta)h)\). For \(h=\cos\theta\), \[Dh=\tfrac12(\cos\theta+3\cos3\theta),\qquad
 \mathcal HDh=-\tfrac12(\sin\theta+3\sin3\theta),\] \[D\mathcal Hh=\tfrac12(\sin\theta-3\sin3\theta).\] Consequently \[\left.\frac{d}{dt}\right|_{t=0}
    (\mathcal B_t^{-1}\mathcal H\mathcal B_t-\mathcal H)\cos\theta=-\sin\theta.\] The printed formula instead gives \(+\frac12\sin\theta\). Equivalently, its coefficient on \(e^{i\theta}\) is \(+\frac{i}{2}e^{-i\theta}\) in place of \(-ie^{-i\theta}\). This witness satisfies the actual spatial-mean and smoothness conditions, and the change is a genuine small diffeomorphism.

Equation reference: eq:A9literal. Appendix A starts from \[T_0=\epsilon_1\Omega\,\partial_\varphi+
       \partial_\theta[(c_{-1}+\epsilon_2f_0)\,\cdot],\] and concludes with full speed \(c_{-1}+\epsilon_2c\). Thus \(c\) is the normalized correction. Part (4) asserts, uniformly in the small parameters, \[
 \left\lVert \Delta c\right\rVert^{\operatorname{Lip}(\lambda)}
 \mathrel{\leq_C}\left\lVert \Delta c_0\right\rVert^{\operatorname{Lip}(\lambda)}
       +\epsilon_2\left\lVert \Delta\rho\right\rVert_{2s_0+2\tau+3}^{\operatorname{Lip}(\lambda)} .\] The proof on the same page initializes \(c_0=0\).

Take \(\Omega=1\), \(c_{-1}=1/2\), and \(f_0(\rho)=\rho\) for constant states \(\rho=r\), with \(r\) small enough that all stated Sobolev norms are at most one. For instance, take \(r_1=1/4\), \(r_2=0\) in the paper’s normalized Fourier norm. The absolute bounds (A.10)–(A.11) hold with \(\sigma_0=0\). For \(\epsilon_1=t\), \(\lambda=t^2\), \(\epsilon_2=t^4\) and fixed sufficiently large \(N_0\), \[\epsilon_1^{-1}\lambda+
       N_0^{\mu_2}\epsilon_2\lambda^{-1}
        =t+N_0^{\mu_2}t^2\longrightarrow0 .\] All smallness conditions (A.13) therefore hold as \(t\to0\). No spatial-mean restriction on \(\rho\) is imposed in this general appendix proposition.

The p. 67 deduction of an ordinary, parameter-uniform bound on \(c_2'\) from (5.6) drops a factor \(\lambda^{-1}\). A correct finite-difference argument uses \(\operatorname{Lip}(c_2)\mathrel{\leq_C}\lambda^{-1}\). In the resonant band \(|j|\le24a\varepsilon^2|\ell|\), with \(a=\omega_0(\xi_*)\) and a fixed compact twist lower bound \(c_{\rm tw}>0\), it gives \[|f_{\ell,j}(\xi)-f_{\ell,j}(\eta)|
 \ge |j|\left(\frac{c_{\rm tw}}{24a}
          -C\varepsilon^2-C\frac{\varepsilon^3}{\lambda}\right)|\xi-\eta|.\] Here \(\varepsilon^3/\lambda=\varepsilon^{1-\delta}\to0\). Thus the lower Lipschitz bound needed for the measure estimate does survive. Each resonant interval has length at most \(C\varepsilon^{2+\delta}|j|^{-1-\tau}\); summing the \(O(|j|\varepsilon^{-2})\) relevant time indices and using \(\tau>1\) gives \(O(\varepsilon^\delta)\). This closes that local loss issue, conditional on the frequency stability and solution inputs. It cannot restore the discrete-exception treatment of the corrected degenerate block.

