For every axis-aligned cube \(Q\) of side length \(r\), the variational bound (2.21), p. 22, implies \[\mathbf b(Q)\le \frac1{|Q|}\int_Q\mathbf a\le I,\qquad
 \mathbf s_*(Q)^{-1}\le\frac1{|Q|}\int_Q\mathbf a^{-1}
 \le 30\max(1,r^{-\alpha})I.\] The scalar estimate follows by integrating the singularity \(|t|^{-\alpha}\) over intervals of length at most one and by covering longer intervals by periods; the one-period average of \(w^{-1}\) is \(5\). In this symmetric case the coarse skew block vanishes. For \(m\ge0\) and \(k\le m\), the inverse-ellipticity term in (1.11) is bounded by \[30\,3^{-\gamma(m-k)}\max(1,3^{-\alpha k})\le30.\] For \(k<0\) this uses \(3^{-\gamma m}3^{(\gamma-\alpha)k}\le1\); for \(k\ge0\) it is immediate. Thus (P2) holds with \(\Lambda_0=1\), \(\lambda_0=1/30\), and \(\mathcal S=1\). One may take \(\Psi_{\mathcal S}(t)=\exp((t-1)_+^2)\) and \(K_{\Psi_{\mathcal S}}=3\): the required tail bound is immediate, and \(\log t+(t-1)^2\le(3t-1)^2\) for \(t\ge1\) proves the growth condition. In particular, \(\nu>\gamma\) is satisfied.

For \(|t|<\varepsilon/2\), [eq:volterra] (#eq:volterra) gives \[v'(t)=c(\varepsilon/2)^\alpha |t|^{-\alpha}
       +O(|t|^{2-2\alpha}),\qquad c>0.\] Take \(s=1/10\) and nonzero nonnegative smooth compactly supported functions \(\psi\) on \((-1,1)\) and \(\eta\) on \((-L,L)\), with \(\int\eta b>0\). For \(0<\delta<\min\{\varepsilon/2,L\}\) let \(\phi_\delta(x)=\psi(x_1/\delta)\eta(x_2)\). Scaling the fractional seminorm yields \[\left\lVert\phi_\delta\right\rVert_{H^s(U)}\le C\delta^{1/2-s},\qquad
 \langle\partial_1u^\varepsilon,\phi_\delta\rangle
   =c_1\delta^{1-\alpha}+o(\delta^{1-\alpha}),\quad c_1>0.\] The constant-coefficient solution \(h\) is smooth on the compact support of these tests, so its pairing is \(O(\delta)\). Consequently \[\frac{|\langle\partial_1(u^\varepsilon-h),\phi_\delta\rangle|}
      {\left\lVert\phi_\delta\right\rVert_{H^s(U)}}
 \ge c_2\delta^{1/2+s-\alpha}\longrightarrow\infty,
 \qquad 1/2+s-\alpha=-1/5.\] These tests belong to \(C_c^\infty(U)\), so the conclusion applies to exactly the dual norm in (1.33), not merely to a boundary-sensitive variant. Volume normalization changes only a constant. Multiplication by the fixed invertible \(\overline{\mathbf s}^{1/2}\) cannot restore membership in \(H^{-s}\). Thus the first term in (1.20) is infinite and its right side is finite. The construction works for every fixed \(\varepsilon>0\), including arbitrarily small \(\varepsilon\). Increasing the finite homogenization scale cannot fix it.

Equation reference: eq:rankone. Let \(t>1\), \(d\ge2\), and take \[s_1=tI_d,\qquad s_{*,1}=I_d,\qquad k_1=\tfrac12(t-1)I_d.\] Tensor the following scalar \(2\)-by-\(2\) matrices with \(I_d\): \[E_1=\begin{pmatrix}(t+1)^2/4&-(t-1)/2\\-(t-1)/2&1\end{pmatrix},
 \quad
 E_{*,1}=\begin{pmatrix}(t+1)^2/(4t)&(t-1)/(2t)\\
                         (t-1)/(2t)&1/t\end{pmatrix}.\] They have precisely the form (2.78), are positive definite, and satisfy \[
 E_1-E_{*,1}
 =\frac{t-1}{4t}
   \begin{pmatrix}t+1\\-2\end{pmatrix}
   \begin{pmatrix}t+1&-2\end{pmatrix}\succeq0.\] The hypotheses allow arbitrary \(k_1\); skew-symmetry cannot be added silently. The contrast \(\widetilde\Theta_1\) in (2.80) is \(t\). The left side of (2.81) is \((t-1)^2\), whereas its claimed upper bound is \(2(t-1)\) for \(t>3\).

One might try to read membership in \(\Omega\) as an implicit additional restriction. Proposition D.3, p. 145, is still false with its stated uniform parameters under that interpretation. For \(0<\theta<\pi/2\), take the deterministic constant field \[g_\theta=\theta J,\qquad h=\pi/2,\quad L=1,\qquad
 \mathbf a_\theta=e^{g_\theta}
 =\cos\theta\,I+\sin\theta\,J.\] The field is stationary and has finite range of dependence in the probabilistic sense, since its law is deterministic. Moreover \(\left\lVert g_\theta\right\rVert\le h\), so the weak \(\Gamma_2\) bound (D.26) holds for every \(\theta\) with the same \(h\). Now \(\operatorname{sym}\mathbf a_\theta=\cos\theta\,I>0\), and the constant field belongs to \(\Omega\).

There is a separate analytic obstruction. Write \(\alpha=d/4-\sigma/2\in(0,d/4)\) and let \[k_\alpha(x)=
 \frac{\Gamma(d/2-\alpha)}
 {4^\alpha\pi^{d/2}\Gamma(\alpha)}
 |x|^{2\alpha-d}\] be the heat-potential kernel from (D.13). The definition immediately before (D.22) integrates a heat convolution with \(\eta_n\); its white-noise kernel is \(k_\alpha*\eta_n\), not the pointwise product \(k_\alpha\eta_n\) written in (D.22). For a nonzero nonnegative smooth annular cutoff, \(k_\alpha*\eta_n\) is strictly positive everywhere and lies in \(L^2\). Its covariance with every translate is strictly positive. The resulting stationary Gaussian field has no finite dependence range. The product kernel, in contrast, is compactly supported. Thus the printed identity does not establish the required property.

