report:
Equation reference: eq:uone, eq:utwo. Take \(k=1\), \(\varepsilon=1\), \(\alpha=3\), all allowed by that literal lemma statement. Then \[\varphi(r)=
 \begin{cases}r,&0\le r<1,\\r^{-2},&r\ge1,\end{cases}
 \qquad
 U(r)=\int_{\mathbb R}\varphi(|y|)\varphi(|y-r|)\,dy.\] The functions are nonnegative and square integrable, so the integrals are legitimate and finite. Splitting at the breakpoints and using symmetry gives \[\begin{aligned}
 U(1)
 &=2\int_1^\infty\frac{dt}{t^2(t+1)^2}
   +2\int_0^1\frac{t\,dt}{(t+1)^2}
   +\int_0^1t(1-t)\,dt\notag\\
 &=\frac{13}{6}-2\log2,\\
 U(2)
 &=2\int_1^\infty\frac{dt}{t^2(t+2)^2}
   +2\int_0^1\frac{t\,dt}{(t+2)^2}
   +2\int_0^1\frac{t\,dt}{(2-t)^2}\notag\\
 &=2+\frac32\log3-4\log2.\end{aligned}\] Consequently \[U(2)-U(1)=\frac12\log(27/16)-\frac16
 >\frac{23}{258}>0.\] The strict bound follows from \(\log x>2(x-1)/(x+1)\) for \(x>1\); differentiating the difference gives \((x-1)^2/[x(x+1)^2]>0\). No floating-point assumption is involved. Independent executed arithmetic also returned approximately \(0.7803723\) and \(0.8753297\) for [eq:uone] (#eq:uone) and [eq:utwo] (#eq:utwo).

The slow-change estimate gives \[\mathcal L(f)^{-\alpha}\mathcal E(\nu)
 \le\mathcal E(f_*\nu)\le\mathcal L(f)^\alpha\mathcal E(\nu).\] If \(\mathbb E\mathcal L^\gamma<C_0\) and \(0<\alpha<\gamma\), then \[C_0^{-\alpha/\gamma}
 <\mathbb E\mathcal L^{-\alpha}
 \le\mathbb E\mathcal L^\alpha
 <C_0^{\alpha/\gamma}.\] The lower bound uses Jensen applied to the reciprocal. These estimates, followed by the energy comparison with additive thresholds, verify the use of Proposition 4.16. They are uniform for the common moment bounds in Theorem 2.8 and do not require continuity of an unbounded moment functional under weak convergence.

Proposition A.1 (PDF pp. 37–38) uses \[A_n=R_{1/(5n)}
 \begin{pmatrix}2^{n^2}&0\\0&2^{-n^2}\end{pmatrix}
 R_{-1/(5n)},\qquad p_n=2^{-n}.\] These are determinant-one matrices with norm \(2^{n^2}\). Since \(\sum_{n\ge1}n^2 2^{-n}=6\), the logarithmic moment is \(6\log2\). Every positive power moment diverges because \(2^{\gamma n^2-n}\) does not tend to zero. Two matrices with distinct eigendirection pairs, for instance \(A_2,A_3\), exclude a common finite projective invariant set, while each is proximal.

