report:
The correct metric-invariant parameter is the volume entropy \(h_X=2\|\rho\|\). The rank-one formula, explicitly stated in [5, p. 920], is \[\lambda_0(\Lambda\backslash X)=
 \begin{cases}
 h_X^2/4,&0\le\delta_\Lambda\le h_X/2,\\
 \delta_\Lambda(h_X-\delta_\Lambda),&h_X/2\le\delta_\Lambda\le h_X .
 \end{cases}
 \tag{3.1}\] For real hyperbolic \(m\)-space of curvature \(-1\), the lattice exponent is \(m-1\), with the cyclic \(m=1\) case giving zero. For complex hyperbolic \(n\)-space in the usual holomorphic-curvature \(-4\) normalization, \(h_X=2n\), whereas its topological boundary has dimension \(2n-1\). The real and complex subgroup exponents in that normalization are \(m-1\) and \(2m\). Metric rescaling must be applied consistently to all of these numbers; it cannot turn linear orbit growth into positive exponential growth.

Take \(\Gamma=\operatorname{PSL}_2(\mathbb Z)\), \(d=1\), and \(\tau^j(z)=z+j\) for positive integers \(j\). The centralizer in \(\Gamma\) is generated by \(\tau\). The displacement formula is \[d(iy,iy+j)=2\operatorname{arsinh}\frac{j}{2y}.\] Thus the displacement-\(<R\) tube has, in the centralizer quotient, area \[\int_0^1\int_{j/(2\sinh(R/2))}^{\infty}\frac{dy\,dx}{y^2}
 =\frac{2\sinh(R/2)}{j}.
 \tag{6.1}\] With \(\operatorname{vol}K=1\), this is the corresponding group-quotient tube volume. The positive powers represent distinct conjugacy classes. Indeed, the gcd of the entries of \(\tau^j-I\) is \(j\) and is invariant under conjugation by integral unimodular matrices; the sign ambiguity in \(\operatorname{PSL}_2\) is removed by taking the trace-two representative.

