As a bound with one absolute \(C\) over the advertised range \(p/q<1/2\), (1.6) is false. Set \[j=3,\qquad q=2y+3,\qquad p=(q-1)/2.\] These are coprime admissible pairs. The leading coefficient of \(e^{2y}\) in (1.4) is \[B_y=\binom{y+2}{2}
       \frac1{16^y\sin^{2y}(\pi/(2q))}
 \geq\binom{y+2}{2}\left(\frac{q^2}{4\pi^2}\right)^y.\] Dividing (1.6) by \(e^{2y}\) and then taking \(e\downarrow0\) would require \(B_y\leq C^{3y+4}\). Its \(y\)th root grows at least quadratically in \(q\), which excludes any fixed \(C\).

For \(\Omega\), center its own intervals at \(\ell-c/q^2\) with half-width \(c/(10q^3)\). Their disjointness follows from the exact positive numerator \[\begin{aligned}
 &10q^3(q+1)^3
 \left(q^{-2}-(q+1)^{-2}
       -\frac{q^{-3}+(q+1)^{-3}}{10}\right)\\
 &\hspace{3em}=18q^3+27q^2+7q-1>0 .\end{aligned}\] Small-period types are excluded by continuity. Lemma 8.8 then makes an endpoint of every sufficiently large \((1,q)\) family visible before \(c\) has been identified.

For a smooth periodic-orbit branch of \(r_e(\phi)=(\cos\phi,\sqrt{1-e^2}\sin\phi)\), stationarity in its vertices gives \[\frac{dL}{de}
 =-e\sum_i
 \frac{(\sin\phi_{i+1}-\sin\phi_i)^2}
 {|r_e(\phi_{i+1})-r_e(\phi_i)|}\leq0 .\] Together with the limiting length \(4\widetilde p\), this proves that hyperbolic families with \(\widetilde p\geq2\) are too long to meet a perimeter at most \(2\pi\). The endpoint identity test for \(\widetilde p=1\) is also consistent: \(E(1/\sqrt2)\leq\pi\sqrt3/4<\sqrt2\). After these boundary checks, fixed-type incidences are locally finite and remaining accumulation can occur only at the locally finite perimeter-incidence set. The resulting set is small, not necessarily itself locally finite. Figure 5 and its numerical values are illustrations, not proofs of this assertion.

