The general assertion for an even lattice of even rank substitutes \(N\)-torsion of its discriminant group for level of its quadratic discriminant form. Take \(L=A_1\oplus A_1\), with Gram matrix \(2I_2\). Its discriminant group has exponent two, but for \(\gamma=(1/2,0)\), \[\theta_\gamma(\tau)=\sum_{m,n\in\mathbb Z}q^{(m+1/2)^2+n^2}
 =2q^{1/4}+\cdots,\qquad
 \theta_\gamma(\tau+2)=-\theta_\gamma(\tau).\] Thus \(T^2\in\Gamma(2)\) acts nontrivially. The correct level is the least \(N\) such that \(N(\gamma,\gamma)/2\in\mathbb Z\) for every discriminant class; here it is four. This is also the condition in [Borcherds, p. 13] and [Retained latex: \cite[p.~5, Propositions 4.3--4.5]{Scheithauer}].

For \(d=\operatorname{len}(\kappa)-3\), the displayed positive volume formula lacks \((-1)^d\). Indeed, for \(y=\operatorname{Im}\tau\) and \(D=(2\pi i)^{-1}\partial_\tau\), \[D^d y^d=\frac{(-1)^d d!}{(4\pi)^d}.\] In the convergent case the zero-vector term therefore gives \[c_0=\frac{(-1)^d d!\mathop{\mathrm{Vol}}(P\Gamma_\kappa\backslash\mathbb{CH}^d)}
               {6(4\pi)^d}.\] A concrete check is \(\kappa=(1^{12})\), \(d=9\). The weighted series is \(C E_{10}\), where \[C=\frac{809}{2^{15}3^{13}25}>0,\qquad
 [q^0]CE_{10}=-\frac{809}{344802322022400}<0.\] This contradicts the printed positive-volume expression. The derivative sign and the corresponding volume formula agree with [Retained latex: \cite[Corollary 4.14, p.~19]{ES}]. This is a constant-term normalization correction; no additional failure of positive-degree enumeration follows. A cusp-regularized weight-two constant requires the separate specification discussed below.

