Consequently \(R_r=m_2(r)/m_1(r)\) converges in law to \(V_2/V_1\). For any \(M<\infty\), Portmanteau gives \[\liminf_{n\to\infty}\mathbb P(R_{2^{-n}}>M)
 \ge \mathbb P(V_2/V_1>M)>0.\] The event \(R_{2^{-n}}>M\) infinitely often belongs to the Brownian germ sigma-field. Its probability is positive, hence one by the germ zero-one law (see [Retained latex: \cite[Corollary 2.2]{sznitman}]). Intersecting over positive integer \(M\) proves \[\limsup_{n\to\infty}
 \frac{\mu_X(B(z_0,2^{1-n}))}
      {\mu_X(B(z_0,2^{-n}))}=\infty
 \quad\hbox{almost surely}.
 \tag{2}\] The eventual identification with \(m_c\) justifies the random terminal-time truncation. This proves failure of the actual measure hypothesis, not merely a missing citation.

In fact \(S'_n\) is eventually empty almost surely. Fix a deterministic \(T<\infty\). If such a time occurs in \([0,T]\), some deterministic grid interval of length \(\delta_n/2\) lies inside \([t,t+\delta_n]\). Relative to its left endpoint, the Brownian path on that grid interval stays within \(2a_n\). Split it into steps of length \(16a_n^2\). Each complete step must have increment at most \(4a_n\) in absolute value. These Gaussian increments are independent, so with \(p_0=\mathbb P(|N(0,1)|\le1)<1\), \[\mathbb P\{\exists t\le T:T_{\pm a_n}(t)>\delta_n\}
 \le
 \left(\frac{2T}{\delta_n}+3\right)
 p_0^{\lfloor\delta_n/(32a_n^2)\rfloor}
 \le C_T n2^n e^{-c2^n/n}.
 \tag{3}\] This is summable. Borel–Cantelli and a countable intersection over integer \(T\) cover the finite random \(T_6\).

For (1), the space \(L_P\) defined in (5.19), page 26, is \(\operatorname{Sym}^3\mathbb C^2\), of dimension four, because the cubes of lines span the symmetric tensor space. At a fixed point the null line \(Z\) has dimension one, so \[\dim(L_P/Z)=3.\] There is one source horizontal CR direction (\(q-1=1\)). The Veronese map is immersive, so the notation of Section 5.2 gives \(J_1=1\). The page-28 equality \[\operatorname{span}\{V_u:u\le J_1\}
 +\operatorname{span}\{h_u^a:u\le J_1,\ a\le q-1\}=L_P\pmod Z
 \tag{5}\] has left side of dimension at most two and right side of dimension three. It is false. This is precisely the degree-two-curve step used to select \(J_2\), establish independence of \(P\), and prove the main technical Lemma 5.4. The subsequent use in Corollary 5.5 and Theorem 1.2 therefore lacks the required proof.

Lemma 50’s covariance conclusion can also be retained with a replacement proof. For \(0<J<I/2\), introduce a joint pair \((\sigma,X)\) with density proportional to \[\exp\{-\tfrac12 a^2X^{\top}(2J)^{-1}X
                  +(v+aX)\cdot\sigma\}.\] The spin marginal is the intended Ising law and \(X\mid\sigma=N(2J\sigma/a,\,2J/a^2)\). Consequently \[a^2\operatorname{Cov}X=2J+4J\operatorname{Cov}(\nu)J.\] The marginal potential of \(X\) has Hessian at least \(a^2((2J)^{-1}-I)\). The Brascamp–Lieb covariance estimate, after a linear change of variables, bounds \(\operatorname{Cov}X\) by its inverse. Substitution and conjugation by \((2J)^{-1}\) give \[\operatorname{Cov}(\nu)\preceq(I-2J)^{-1}
       \preceq(1-2\|J\|_{\rm OP})^{-1}I.\] This avoids the unjustified claim on p.61 that a componentwise nonlinear contraction necessarily contracts covariance in Loewner order. The missing external field in the marginal normalizer and the extra factor in the p.60 exponent are local corrections. Integrating this bound yields Corollary 51’s \((1-2\|J\|_{\rm OP})/n\); its mixing constant depends on the distance from \(1/2\).

Theorem 58 does not require entropy annealing. With \(C_t=I\), strong convexity gives \(\|\operatorname{Cov}(\nu_t)\|\le(\mu+t)^{-1}\); Proposition 39 and Proposition 19 then give \[\rho_{\rm LS}(P)\ge
   \exp\!\left(-\int_0^{1/\eta}\frac{dt}{\mu+t}\right)
   =\frac{\mu}{\mu+1/\eta}.\] The Gaussian conditional center correction above is needed in the identification of the oracle. The following step-count prose on p.38 has the factor reversed. Put \(a=\eta\mu\). For an initial density bounded by \(\beta\ge1\), entropy contraction and Pinsker give \[D_{\mathrm{KL}}(\rho_k\Vert\nu)\le(1+a)^{-k}\log\beta,\qquad
 \|\rho_k-\nu\|_{\rm TV}
 \le\sqrt{\tfrac12(1+a)^{-k}\log\beta}.\] Consequently, when \(0<a<1\), a sufficient number of steps is \[O\!\left(a^{-1}
 \{\log\max(1,\log\beta)+\log(1/\epsilon)+1\}\right).\] For the source’s \(O(\exp n)\)-warm starts this is \(O((\eta\mu)^{-1}(\log n+\log(1/\epsilon)+1))\). The theorem’s coefficient itself is correct.

