For \(\varepsilon\leq1/4\) the cutoff weight is one on these cubes. After cancellation of the affine domain-coordinate Jacobian, the signed density on an inner cube is \[\frac{a_n^3(2\pi m_n)^3
       \prod_{j=1}^3\cos(2\pi m_nU_j)}
      {(1+a_n\sin(2\pi m_nU_3))^3}\,dU_1dU_2dU_3 .\] The positive and negative parts each have integral at least \[\frac{32a_n^3m_n^3}{(1+a_n)^3}.\] Indeed, the integral of the positive part of the product of three cosines on the unit cube is \(4/\pi^3\), and the negative part has the same integral. Here \(a_n^3m_n^3=2^{3n}/10^6\), so both sign integrals diverge. Contributions in the cutoff transition regions cannot remove these lower bounds. Thus the ordinary signed Lebesgue integral is undefined as \(\infty-\infty\), despite all the printed hypotheses.

The real first-variation formula gives \[V=-4\,\frac{\overline{\mathcal S(g_\mu)}}{\rho_{\mathbb D^*}},
 \qquad dS(V)=-\|V\|_{\mathrm{WP}}^2.\] On \(T_0(1)\) the smooth Kähler potential and strong Hilbert metric give a locally well-posed gradient equation. The universal WP metric is Cauchy complete [Retained latex: \cite[Corollary 5.3, p.~29]{GMR}]; this differs from the finite-genus situation. If a maximal forward curve had finite lifetime \(T\), then, for \(s<t<T\), \[d_{\mathrm{WP}}(\alpha(s),\alpha(t))
 \leq\sqrt{t-s}
 \left(\int_s^t\|V\|_{\mathrm{WP}}^2\,du\right)^{1/2}
 \leq\sqrt{t-s}\sqrt{S(\alpha(0))}.\] It is Cauchy, reaches an endpoint by completeness, and extends by the local Hilbert ODE. This supports forward global existence on \(T_0(1)\) independently of the ambient flow claim.

The key dependency chain is \[\text{brick discretization}
 \Longrightarrow\text{Theorem 5.9}
 \Longrightarrow\text{robust-cycle obstruction (6.4)}
 \Longrightarrow\text{Theorems 1.1--1.2}.\] The minimality/turbulence argument in Section 7 and the countable classification of one-dimensional chains in Appendix A have distinct proof obligations. A defect in the latter does not, by itself, disprove the robust-cycle obstruction.

Lemma A.3, also on p. 33, needs compatible counting language. Arbitrarily many disjoint subsegments can be selected inside one horizontal block, so mere pairwise Euclidean separation does not count maximal horizontal pieces. For a correct closed-witness construction, require \(n\) horizontal segments of length at least \(1/m\) whose heights differ pairwise by at least \(1/m\). Their union over \(m\) counts distinct maximal horizontal pieces and is \(F_\sigma\). Use distinct horizontal coordinates for vertical pieces. Since distinct strict pieces are separated by a flat piece, \[\Phi^\infty=\bigcap_{n\geq1}
   \bigl(\{\text{at least }n\text{ maximal horizontal pieces}\}
       \cup\{\text{at least }n\text{ maximal vertical pieces}\}\bigr).\] This gives the required Borel partition, indeed the advertised \(\Pi^0_3\) bound, without counting subdivisions of one block. It is included in the same measurability repair, not counted as a duplicate core failure.

For a non-archimedean embedding, the Tate lift is multiplication by an integer \(m\) dividing \(M\), with either sign permitted. Pullback of \(du/u\) gives \[aF(s_1)=mF(s_2).\] Thus \[P_{\rm fin}(Y_1,Y_2)=
       \prod_{\substack{m\in\mathbb Z\setminus\{0\}\\m\mid M}}
                  (aY_1-mY_2)\] is nonzero, homogeneous, and has degree \(2d(M)\). The scalar \(a\) is algebraic and fixed independently of the embedding. No factor indexed by an unbounded set of places is introduced. Cyclicity is unnecessary for this particular step. The toric case \(q_1=q_2=0\) in Proposition 4.6 can be handled directly by the group maps \(z\mapsto z^m\); the later elliptic application has nonzero Tate parameters.

The additional rational G-function \[H(X)=\frac{\zeta}{\zeta-X}\] enforces \[R'_\iota=\min\{1,R(H^\iota),R(F_\lambda^\iota),
                        R(G_\lambda^\iota):\lambda\in\Lambda\}
       \leq r_\iota.\] Weak approximation can make \(\zeta\) sufficiently small at the finitely many constrained embeddings. At every remaining embedding, the minimum with one already supplies the required bound. No interpretation of a non-archimedean value of \(G_\lambda\) is used to prove the local vanishing.

Let \[P=\{u\ge0,\ v\ge0,\ u+v\le3\}
   =\operatorname{conv}\{0,a,b\},
 \quad a=(2,-1,-1),\quad b=(1,1,-2),\] and let \(Q=P+(1,-1,0)\). Both are integral triangles in \(H\). Their three facet normals are roots, up to signs: \(e_1-e_2\), \(e_2-e_3\), and \(e_1-e_3\). The translation changes \((u,v)\) by \((2,-1)\). Consequently \[P\cap Q=\{u\ge2,\ v\ge0,\ u+v\le3\}.\] Its vertex \((u,v)=(2,0)\) is \[(4/3,-2/3,-2/3),\] which is not integral. This is a bounded, explicit counterexample to statement (B) in the remark’s asserted alcoved setting.

