   =(t+10)\sum_{a\in\mathcal A}\frac1{10(a+t)+1}.
 \tag{2.2}\] All evaluations at \(t=j/400\), \(0\leq j\leq100\), and the global derivative bound were computed as exact fractions. The minimum grid value exceeds \(1.00471887\), and an upper bound for the absolute derivative is \(0.07920660\). Every point of \(I\) is within \(1/800\) of a grid point, giving the certified lower bound \[\inf_I\frac{\mathcal L_{1/2}h}{h}
       >1.00461986>\frac{1003}{1000}.\] The displayed decimals are outward-safe summaries of saved rational inequalities. The exact integer comparison \((1001/1000)^{5000}>129\), together with \(a+t<129\), yields \[\frac{\mathcal L_{0.5001}h}{h}
   >\frac{1003}{1001}>1.\] The finite-system pressure criterion therefore gives \(\delta_{\mathcal A}>0.5001>1/2\). For the thirty-one-letter initial alphabet \(\{4,8,\ldots,124\}\), the exact maximum grid value plus the derivative allowance is less than \(0.99809483<999/1000<1\). Its pressure at \(s=1/2\) is negative. Any alphabet with at most thirty-one positive multiples of four has ordered entries at least the corresponding entries of this initial alphabet. Continuant polynomials have positive coefficients, so corresponding word derivatives and pressures are bounded above by those of the initial alphabet. This also verifies the cardinality threshold of thirty-two. The saved rational certificates do not reproduce the source’s twelve-decimal dimension interval. A simpler two-letter minimum-derivative sum was also tried; its value is below one and was correctly rejected as a certificate.

