In the exact expansion \(B_+=b_{++}F_++b_{+-}F_-\), taking a Wronskian with \(F_-\) gives \[b_{++}
  =\frac{W(B_+,F_-)}{W(F_+,F_-)}
  =c^{-1}\left[e^{i\pi/4}
                  +O(e^{-\kappa_+A_0}+\mathbb L^{-1})\right].\] The displayed (5.45) instead asserts \[cb_{++}=e^{-i\pi/4}+O(e^{-\kappa_+A_0}).\] The normalized leading discrepancy is exactly \[|e^{i\pi/4}-e^{-i\pi/4}|=\sqrt2.\] Take, for example, the admissible fixed background \(M=m=1,e=0\). The source’s convention allows the matching parameter \(A_0\) large and then \(\mathbb L\) sufficiently large relative to it, with \(k\) in the stated threshold regime. The unqualified error constants in (5.45) depend on the background, not on \(A_0\); neighboring estimates explicitly use an \(A_0\) subscript when that dependence is allowed. Thus the errors can be made arbitrarily small, while the discrepancy remains \(\sqrt2\). This calculation isolates the actual coefficient and does not depend on a choice of observation point where two oscillations might cancel.

Fix the background, the spatial cutoff \(S_0\), the required large weight exponent, \(R>r_+\), and \(\check\delta>0\). Choose \(s_0<S_0\) with \(r(s_0)\in(r_+,R)\), and \[\chi\in C_c^\infty((-\infty,S_0)),\quad \chi(s_0)=1,\qquad
 0\ne\eta\in C_c^\infty((m+1,m+2)),\quad\eta\geq0.\] Take a fixed finite \(t_0\geq1\) such that \(T_0=t_0+p(s_0)\geq1\). Choose a sufficiently large fixed \(L_0\) so that \(t_0,T_0<\exp(\check\delta\bar L_0\log\bar L_0)\). Set \[\begin{split}
 u_0(\omega,L,s)&=1_{\{L=L_0\}}
                 e^{i\omega t_0}\eta(\omega)\chi(s),\\
 H_0(\omega,L,s)&=(\partial_s^2-V)u_0(\omega,L,s).
 \end{split}\] The equation holds exactly. Spatial support is compact and below \(S_0\); frequency support is compact and away from \(0,m\). All required derivatives are smooth. Only one finite angular mode is used, and \((r-r_+)^{-1}\) is bounded on the source support. Thus \(0<\mathcal D[H_0]<\infty\): if \(H_0\) were zero, uniqueness for the homogeneous ODE and the vanishing solution outside its compact support would force \(u_0=0\).

At the selected observation point, \[\int_0^\infty e^{-iT_0\omega}e^{i\omega p(s_0)}
       u_0(\omega,L_0,s_0)\,d\omega
   =\int_0^\infty\eta(\omega)\,d\omega=:c_0>0.\] For \(u_\varepsilon=\varepsilon u_0\) and \(H_\varepsilon=\varepsilon H_0\), the source support and every fixed parameter remain unchanged, but \[\mathcal D[H_\varepsilon]=\varepsilon^2\mathcal D[H_0].\] The indicator term in Proposition 6.6 is zero at \(t_0\). Its remaining bound would therefore imply \(\varepsilon c_0\leq C\varepsilon^2\mathcal D[H_0]\), with \(C\) finite and independent of \(\varepsilon\). Letting \(\varepsilon\downarrow0\) gives a contradiction.

