Put \[F(s)=\sum_{\mathfrak d}
 \frac{\mu(\mathfrak d)\rho_v(\mathfrak d)}{\mathrm N(\mathfrak d)^s}
 =\zeta_K(s)^{-1}G(s).\] The legitimate Euler grouping is by rational primes. Write \(\widetilde{\mathfrak S}\) for the product in (9.4). For \(p\ne5\), the splitting types in this field give the following ratios between the correct local factor of \(G(1)\) and the paper’s factor: \[\begin{array}{c|c}
 p\bmod5&\text{ratio}\\ \hline
 1&1+(p-1)^{-3}\\
 4&1+(p-1)/(p^2-1)^2\\
 2,3&1 .
 \end{array}\] For the type \(2+2\), divisibility by either degree-two prime imposes two conditions and divisibility by both imposes only three. The actual factor is \(1-2p^{-2}+p^{-3}\), yielding the displayed ratio. For an inert prime and for \(p=5\) there is only one prime ideal, so no cross-prime-ideal factor is missing. These extra factors form an absolutely convergent product, all are at least one, and the factor at 11 is strictly larger than one. Consequently \[G(1)>\widetilde{\mathfrak S}.\] The Euler product after cancellation of its degree-one terms converges in a neighborhood of \(s=1\); this justifies the value and continuity used here.

Define the sum in Lemma 9.5 for the chosen \(m,I\) by \[S(R)=\sum_{\mathrm N(\mathfrak d)<R}
 \frac{\mu(\mathfrak d)\rho_v(\mathfrak d)}{\mathrm N(\mathfrak d)}
 \log\frac R{\mathrm N(\mathfrak d)}.\] For \(u>0\), termwise integration in the absolutely convergent half-plane gives \[F(1+u)=u^2\int_1^\infty S(R)R^{-u-1}\,dR .\] If Lemma 9.5 held, \(S(R)\to\widetilde{\mathfrak S}/\gamma_K\), so the Abelian identity would imply \(\lim_{u\downarrow0}F(1+u)/u=\widetilde{\mathfrak S}/\gamma_K\). The simple pole of \(\zeta_K\), however, gives \[\lim_{u\downarrow0}\frac{F(1+u)}u=\frac{G(1)}{\gamma_K}
 >\frac{\widetilde{\mathfrak S}}{\gamma_K},\] a contradiction. No unproved replacement Tauberian theorem is needed for this refutation of the stated constant.

With \(P_1=X^\tau,P_2=X^{\tau'}\), the prime reciprocal sum has main term \[\frac2{\varphi(D_f)}
 \log\frac{\log P_2}{\log P_1}
 =\frac2{\varphi(D_f)}\log\frac{\tau'}{\tau},\] rather than \(2\log(P_2/P_1)/\varphi(D_f)\). This follows by partial summation from the prime number theorem in the fixed progression. Theorem 4.1 itself has the correct \(\log(\tau'/\tau)\), so this is an intermediate normalization error, not a counterexample to its final constant.

