report:
The exact identity central to Section 5 is \[\lambda_0(V_\varepsilon,v_0)_{L^2}
      =2\mathcal E_\varepsilon-2L_\varepsilon(v_0).\] It follows by combining the crack boundary terms with the equation for \(\psi_\varepsilon=v_0-V_\varepsilon\). The complementary spectral gap is uniform for sufficiently small \(\varepsilon\), since the limiting eigenvalue is simple. The spectral residual gives \(|\lambda_\varepsilon-\lambda_0|=O(\|V_\varepsilon\|_{L^2})\) and \(\|\psi_\varepsilon-\Pi_\varepsilon\psi_\varepsilon\|_{\mathcal H_\varepsilon}
 =O(\|V_\varepsilon\|_{L^2})\). Normalization then gives \(\|\widehat v_\varepsilon-v_0\|_{L^2}=O(\|V_\varepsilon\|_{L^2})\). Substitution in (5.17) yields the stated expansion, with an error bounded by \(O(\|V_\varepsilon\|_{L^2}^2)\), hence \(o(\|V_\varepsilon\|_{\mathcal H_\varepsilon}^2)\). If \(V_\varepsilon=0\) at an exceptional parameter, the weak equation already makes \(v_0\) a perturbed eigenfunction; divisions by its norm in a contradiction argument are needed only on a subsequence with \(V_\varepsilon\ne0\).

On \(X=(\mathbb C/(\mathbb Z+i\mathbb Z))^2\), for \(s>0\) put \[\Omega_s=\frac{i}{2}
   \left(s\,dz_1\wedge d\bar z_1+
                  s^{-1}dz_2\wedge d\bar z_2\right).\] These distinct flat metrics have \(\theta=0\), \(d\Omega_s=\theta\wedge\Omega_s\), \(\nabla\theta=0\), \(c_1^{\mathrm{BC}}=0\), the same Lee class, and the same volume. Thus the self-contained printed definition admits them, whereas Theorem B(1) asserts uniqueness. Moreover \(\operatorname{Aut}_0(X)=X\) has complex dimension two, contradicting the literal one-dimensional conclusion of Theorem C.

