Equation reference: walk. Let \(m=2^{17}\) and \(N=m-1=131071\). On \(\Omega=\{-1,1\}^{N}\) with the uniform probability measure let \(\varepsilon_1,\ldots,\varepsilon_N\) be the coordinate signs. Define the absorbed random walk \[
 X_0=1,\qquad X_k=X_{k-1}+\varepsilon_k\mathbf 1_{\{X_{k-1}>0\}}
 \quad(1\le k\le N).\] Take \(\mathcal M=L^\infty(\Omega)\), the probability trace \(\tau(f)=\mathbb Ef\), and the coordinate filtration \(\mathcal M_k\), extended constantly after time \(N\). This is a finite, hence semifinite, von Neumann algebra with a normal faithful trace. The filtration has union \(\mathcal M\) and the ordinary trace-preserving conditional expectations. In particular, no issue concerning an unbounded trace or existence of conditional expectations enters this example.

Equation reference: reflection. Let \(S_r\) denote an unabsorbed symmetric random walk started at zero. For \(j>0\) of the appropriate parity, reflecting a path after its first visit to zero gives \[\begin{aligned}
 \mathbb P(X_N=j)
 &=2^{-N}\left\{
 \binom{N}{(N+j-1)/2}-\binom{N}{(N+j+1)/2}\right\}\notag\\
 &=\frac{2j}{N+1}\mathbb P(S_{N+1}=j).\end{aligned}\] The first binomial counts all paths from \(1\) to \(j\); the second counts the paths that visit zero, by their bijection with paths from \(-1\) to \(j\). An out-of-range binomial coefficient is zero. The second equality follows directly from the two adjacent binomial coefficients. One may define \(S_{N+1}\) on an auxiliary fair-sign space; only its distribution is used here.

Equation reference: lower. Symmetry and [reflection] (#reflection) give the exact identity \[\mathbb EX_N^4=\frac{\mathbb E|S_{N+1}|^5}{N+1}.\] Since \(\mathbb ES_{N+1}^2=N+1\), Jensen’s inequality for \(u^{5/2}\) yields \[
 \|y_N\|_4=(\mathbb EX_N^4)^{1/4}
 \ge (N+1)^{3/8}=2^{51/8}>80.\] The strict comparison is entirely integral: \(2^{51}>80^8\) is equivalent to \(2^{19}>5^8\), namely \(524288>390625\).

