report:
Lemma 4.1.2, page 31, uses the convention that the cyclotomic character has Hodge–Tate weight \(-1\). The weights of the ordinary subrepresentation and quotient are \[\begin{array}{c|cc}
 & V^+ & V^-\\ \hline
\mathfrak p & -j-r & -j-1+r\\
\overline{\mathfrak p} & j-r & j-1+r .
\end{array}\] For \(0\leq j<r\), the two \(V^+\) weights are negative and the two quotient weights are nonnegative. At the endpoint \(j=r-1\), the quotient weight at \(\mathfrak p\) is zero, so it remains on the nonnegative side. For \(j\geq r\), both weights at \(\mathfrak p\) are negative, and both at \(\overline{\mathfrak p}\) are nonnegative; at \(j=r\) the latter subrepresentation has weight zero. These checks agree with \[\operatorname{Sel}_{\rm BK}
=\begin{cases}
\operatorname{Sel}_{\rm ord,ord},&0\leq j<r,\\
\operatorname{Sel}_{\rm rel,str},&j\geq r.
\end{cases}\] The equality uses the cited local comparison results, not the weight table alone. Their de Rham and exceptional-invariant hypotheses have not been independently re-proved here. A weight-zero endpoint by itself is not a demonstrated counterexample.

The proof of Lemma 3.3 on page 15 places the centres \(x_a\) on the sphere of radius \(r_{j+1}/10\), in a rotation orbit, with disjoint annuli \(A_{r_j,2r_j}(x_a)\) and \(r_{j+1}\gg r_j\gg1\). It asserts a function \(\eta\) on the complement of the inner balls with, among other properties, \[\begin{aligned}
\eta(x)&=|x-x_0|^2 &&\text{on }A_{r_j,2r_j}(x_0),\\
\eta(x)&=|x|^2 &&\text{outside }B_{100r_j}(0).\end{aligned}\] The subscript in the latter radius is \(j\) in both layout and raw extraction of the bound page.

Use the permitted even value \(k=2\), the permitted endpoint \(\beta=1\), and the regular point \(\psi=\pi/2\). The fibre length is then constant one. Equations (60)–(62), (90), (95), and (104) give the actual orthonormal two-dimensional block \[R=\begin{pmatrix}
2 & \displaystyle\frac{2\alpha k}{\sqrt{1+\alpha^2k^2}}\\[5pt]
\displaystyle\frac{2\alpha k}{\sqrt{1+\alpha^2k^2}}&
\displaystyle\frac{\alpha^{-2}+2\alpha^2k^2}{1+\alpha^2k^2}
\end{pmatrix}.\] Here the horizontal contraction of the curvature form in the \(F_2\) direction vanishes, as does the Hessian of the constant fibre length. Direct calculation yields \[\det(\alpha^2R)=\frac{2\alpha^2}{1+\alpha^2k^2}.\] For \(k=2\), \[\frac12-\det(\alpha^2R)=\frac{1}{2(1+4\alpha^2)}>0.\] The assertion therefore fails for every positive \(\alpha\), including arbitrarily small values below any proposed threshold. At \(\alpha=1/10\), the determinant is exactly \(1/52\). This uses the paper’s metric family and all of the relevant hypotheses, not an unrelated matrix with a similar asymptotic form.

There is a related exact-formula error in (106)–(107), page 31. Write \[c=(1-\beta)k,\qquad
f=\sqrt{1+\alpha^2k^2\sin^2\psi},\qquad
u=\sqrt{1+\alpha^2c^2\sin^2\psi}.\] The base still uses \(k\), even though the circle-fibre length is now \(u\). The metric on a two-sphere fibre in the base has meridian length element \(\alpha\,d\psi\) and rotational profile \[a(\psi)=\frac{\alpha\sin\psi}{f}.\] Its fibres are totally geodesic. For a function of \(\psi\), direct differentiation gives \[\begin{aligned}
\frac{\operatorname{Hess}u(F_1,F_1)}{u}
 &=\frac{c^2\cos^2\psi}{u^4}-\frac{c^2\sin^2\psi}{u^2},\\
\frac{\operatorname{Hess}u(F_2,F_2)}{u}
 &=\frac{1}{\alpha^2u}\frac{a'}a\,u'
 =\frac{c^2\cos^2\psi}{u^2f^2}.\end{aligned}\] The second coefficient in (107) instead has denominator \(u^4\). Their difference, actual minus printed, is \[-\frac{\alpha^2c^2(k^2-c^2)\sin^2\psi\cos^2\psi}{u^4f^2}.\] For \(0<\beta<1\), nonzero \(k\), and an interior latitude this is generally nonzero. For example, with \(k=2,\beta=1/2,\psi=\pi/4,\alpha=1/10\), the actual and printed coefficients are respectively \(5000/10251\) and \(20000/40401\). Consequently the corresponding Laplacian identity (106) must also be corrected. These derivative identities were independently checked symbolically. The endpoint determinant counterexample above is unaffected by this additional error, since \(u=1\) there.

For the matrix \(R-I\), \(C-1>0\) at small \(\alpha\), and its Schur complement is \[A-1-\frac{B^2}{C-1}
=A-1-\frac{(\alpha B)^2}{\alpha^2C-\alpha^2}
=1+O_k(\alpha^2).\] It is uniformly positive for sufficiently small \(\alpha\). This proves the required positivity of this block by a valid argument. The separate \(Z_2,Z_3\) and \(F_1\) directions retain the lower bounds used on page 32; their small-\(\alpha\) limits or dominant \(\alpha^{-2}\) terms are consistent with the corrected calculation.

