For \(i>\ell\), \(R_2\) gives \(y_{n-i}^2\leq i^{1/4}\) and \(s_{n-i}^2\geq i^{3/2}/n\), once \(n\) is large enough that \(\ell^{1/8}\geq\log\log n\). Consequently \[0\leq \widetilde\chi^2-\chi^2
 \leq1+n\sum_{i>\ell}i^{-5/4}
 \leq1+4n\ell^{-1/4}.\] Together with [eq:lower] (#eq:lower), this yields \[\frac{\widetilde\chi^2-\chi^2}{\chi^2}
 =O\!\left((\log\log n)^5
       \big((\log n)^{-1/8}+n^{-1}\big)\right)
 =o((\log n)^{-1/16}).\] The additional deterministic loss \(\varepsilon^{1/4}\) is smaller than the displayed logarithmic error in the intended range. This verifies the deterministic content of Lemma 4.3. It does not prove that the random event \(R^c\) has the small joint probability asserted in Lemma 4.2; that requires Sections 5–7 and their external inputs.

On a boundary face with a zero eigenvalue and all eigenvalues bounded away from one, the limiting product is zero. At a vertex where \(\mu_1\to1\), write \(\delta=1-\mu_1\), so that \(\sum_{j>1}\mu_j\leq\delta\). The arithmetic–geometric mean inequality gives \[F(\mu)\leq
 \frac{(\delta/(n-1))^{(n-1)/2}}
      {\delta(1-\delta)^{n-1}}.\] Its upper limit is zero for \(n>3\) and at most \(1/2\) for \(n=3\). In the latter case the isotropic value is \(\sqrt{27}/8>1/2\), since \(27/64>1/4\). This proves (*) and the uniqueness of its maximum, including the boundary cases which require \(n\geq3\).

Assessment: Major, quantitative proof gap. Theorem 5.1 (p. 25) claims, for \(\delta>0\), \[\#\{y\in Y_N^*:|P_f(y)|\le(\log N)^{1/2-\delta}\}
 \ll \frac{N^2}{(\log N)^{1+\min(\delta,1/4)}}.\] Its proof sets \(\alpha=1+\min(\delta,1/4)\). Equation (5.2) bounds the mass of the real standard Gaussian on \([-r,r]\), \(r=c''(\log N)^{-(\alpha-1)}\), by \(O((\log N)^{-2(\alpha-1)})\). The actual one-dimensional density satisfies \[\int_{-r}^{r}\frac{e^{-x^2/2}}{\sqrt{2\pi}}\,dx
 =\sqrt{\frac2\pi}\,r+O(r^3).\] Since \(\alpha>1\), its ratio to the proposed squared-radius bound is unbounded. For example, at \(\delta=1/8\) the mass has order \((\log N)^{-1/8}\), not \(O((\log N)^{-1/4})\). The error \(O((\log N)^{-1/2})\) in Theorem 3.4 cannot repair this mismatch. Both the printed integral and the real one-dimensional input were checked in the PDF (pp. 17, 25). A two-dimensional Gaussian estimate does not apply to these Maaß periods.

Assessment: Major, missing condition in the weighted-transfer route. For the definition (6.1), p. 28, let \(I=\{x\in X:\deg(x)=0\}\). Direct summation gives \[G(\mu)(Y)
 =\sum_{\deg(x)>0}\sum_{y\in e(x)}\frac{\mu(x)}{\deg(x)}
 =1-\mu(I).\] Thus this is a probability measure only if \(\mu(I)=0\). The definition \(e^{-1}(B)=\{x:e(x)\subset B\}\) on p. 18 includes isolated vertices. Taking \(B=Y\) in Lemma 6.1 would therefore give \(1\le 1-\mu(I)\), which is false when they carry positive mass. The observation that a vertex adjacent to a given \(y\) has positive degree does not exclude isolated vertices elsewhere.

