Add a second, plus-phase ball centered at \(x_0=(3,0,0)\), of radius \(r_\varepsilon=\sqrt\varepsilon\), inside the minus phase. Use the same smooth profile across this sphere, with thickness of order \(\varepsilon\), and truncate its tails while staying away from the principal interface and the outer boundary. The calibration \(\xi\) vanishes near the small ball. Its additional energy and bulk error satisfy \[E_{\varepsilon,\mathrm{drop}}
  =O(r_\varepsilon^2+\varepsilon r_\varepsilon+\varepsilon^2)=O(\varepsilon),\qquad
 B_{\mathrm{drop}}=O(r_\varepsilon^3+\varepsilon r_\varepsilon^2)=O(\varepsilon^{3/2}).\] Exponential tail errors do not change these bounds. The values remain on the bounded closest segment. Consequently the full preparation condition (1.15) holds with a uniform constant. These are smooth compatible initial data for (1.7).

Let \(R=\sum_\pm\int_{\Omega_0^\pm}|\nabla u_0^\pm|^2>0\). The construction is the exact original bulk map on the support of those gradients, where \(F=0\) and \(\nabla\psi=0\). Its contribution to \(E_\varepsilon/\varepsilon\) is exactly \(R/2\). The optimal normal layer contributes \(O(\varepsilon)\) after this division, and the truncated tails tend to zero exponentially. Thus \[\lim_{\varepsilon\downarrow0}\frac{E_\varepsilon[u_\varepsilon^{\rm in}|\Sigma_0]}{\varepsilon}
    =\frac12\sum_\pm\int_{\Omega_0^\pm}
                  |\nabla u_0^\pm|^2
    =\frac R2,\] whereas (5.40) prints the sum without the factor \(1/2\). This is also the coefficient carried by the preceding formula (5.39). Restoring it does not alter the \(O(\varepsilon)\) preparation estimate.

