abla\psi_\varepsilon\cdot H
             +\varepsilon^2|\Pi_{u_\varepsilon}
abla u_\varepsilon|^2|H|^2.\] It replaces this by \(|c|g|n_\varepsilon+\varepsilon|\Pi_{u_\varepsilon}
abla u_\varepsilon|H|^2\). Where \(
abla\psi_\varepsilon=0\), the printed convention is \(n_\varepsilon=0\). If \(g
eq0\) and \(\Pi_{u_\varepsilon}
abla u_\varepsilon=0\), the first expression is \(c^2|g|^2\) and the proposed square is zero. The chain rule does not remove that discrepancy.

A bounded correction is explicit. Choose an arbitrary unit normal where \(\nabla\psi_\varepsilon=0\), as in Fischer–Laux–Simon [FLS], (11a), or retain the omitted nonnegative remainder. On that zero-gradient set, \[\frac{c^2|g|^2}{2\varepsilon}
