Equation reference: eq:slice. Use the standard ensemble \((\mathbb R^4,S^3,\iota)\), with \(\iota(r,\theta)=r\theta\), \(k^*=30\), and \(\alpha=1/2\). Its topological conditions hold as above. Let \(\gamma_0=dr^2+r^2h_0\) be the Euclidean cone. Its scalar curvature is zero, so \(\gamma_0\in\operatorname{Cone}_{R\geq0}^{k^*}(S^3)\). Choose five nonzero smooth functions \(\phi_j\) on \(S^3\) with pairwise disjoint supports and \(0\leq\phi_j\leq1\), and set \[e_j=r^2\phi_j h_0,\qquad 1\leq j\leq5 .\] They are linearly independent elements of \(T\operatorname{Cone}^{k^*}\). For sufficiently small \(x,y\), define \[\begin{aligned}
 F_1(x,y)&=\gamma_0+xe_1+ye_2,\\
 F_2(x,y)&=\gamma_0+xe_1+ye_2+x^2e_3+xye_4+y^2e_5. \end{aligned}\] Both maps are polynomial into the \(C^{30}\) Banach space and hence real-analytic. Their projections onto the first two coefficients are the identity, so they are embeddings, with common tangent space \(E=\operatorname{span}(e_1,e_2)\) at \(\gamma_0\). They are orientable by \((x,y)\). Disjoint supports and \(|x|,|y|<\varepsilon<1/2\) make the angular metric positive definite. Their images are therefore contained in \(\operatorname{GenCone}\) (in fact in \(\operatorname{Cone}\)). The definitions before Lemma 10.36, pp. 105–106, do not require all of \(Q_i\) to have nonnegative scalar curvature. The required intersection with \(\operatorname{Cone}_{R\geq0}\) contains \(\gamma_0\).

