report:
Pages 9–10 assume a multiplier bound for every \(\theta\in[0,\theta_0]\), with \(A\ge2\) and fixed \(\theta_0>0\). After duality, two factors are estimated in \(L^\infty\). Cauchy–Schwarz gives, for \(0<\theta\le\theta_0\), \[\left\|A^{\theta/2}\langle D\rangle^{-1/2-\theta}g\right\|_\infty
 \le C A^{\theta/2}
 \left(\sum_{k\in\mathbb Z}\langle k\rangle^{-1-2\theta}\right)^{1/2}
 \|g\|_2
 \le C_{\theta_0}A^{\theta/2}\theta^{-1/2}\|g\|_2 .\] Taking \(\theta=\min\{\theta_0,1/\log A\}\) yields \(C_{\theta_0}\sqrt{\log A}\) per factor, hence the stated \(\log A\) bound. The choice never uses \(\theta=0\), where the displayed summation would diverge. Dependence on the fixed \(\theta_0\) is essential and is compatible with this lemma.

Let \(I(z)=\int_0^1e^{izr}\,dr\), with \(I(0)=1\), and put \[E=I(\tau(\alpha+\beta))-I(\tau\alpha)I(\tau\beta).\] This is precisely the averaged difference on pp. 11–12. The covariance identity and oscillation bounds give \(|E|\le2\min\{\tau|\alpha|,\tau|\beta|\}\). For \(\alpha\ne0\), integration by parts gives \[\begin{aligned}
 E={}&\frac{e^{i\tau\alpha}(e^{i\tau\beta}-1)}{i\tau\alpha}
 -\frac{\beta}{\alpha}I(\tau(\alpha+\beta)) \nonumber\\
 &-I(\tau\alpha)\frac1\tau\int_0^\tau(e^{i\beta s}-1)\,ds .\end{aligned}\] Using \(|e^{iz}-1|\le |z|\), \(|I(z)|\le1\), and \(|I(\tau\alpha)|\le2/(\tau|\alpha|)\), the three terms are bounded by \(|\beta/\alpha|\), \(|\beta/\alpha|\), and \(|\beta/\alpha|\), respectively. Symmetry therefore proves the ratio part of (3.8), with an absolute constant.

For \(\alpha+\beta\ne0\), assume \(|\alpha|\ge|\beta|\); then \[|E|\le \frac{2}{\tau|\alpha+\beta|}
           +\frac{2}{\tau|\alpha|}
       \le\frac{6}{\tau|\alpha+\beta|}.\] The other ordering is symmetric. If one phase is zero, \(E=0\). If \(\beta=-\alpha\ne0\), the second estimate is not asserted: instead \(E=1-|I(\tau\alpha)|^2\). Thus this resonant boundary is consistent with the actual hypotheses. No spurious division by zero is required in the checked proof.

The last stage on pp. 43–44 is consistent provided the preceding stability and remainder estimates, including their localized versions, are available. On an interval of length \(h\) with \(\delta=h+\tau\le1\), the choice \(N=\delta^{-1/4}\ge1\) gives \[hN^2\le\delta^{1/2}\le\delta^{1/4},
 \qquad N^{-23/14}=\delta^{23/56}\le\delta^{1/4}.\] Under the bootstrap \(E\le2\tau^{\gamma/2}\), \[|\log(1/\tau)|^{96}E^{96}
 \le 2^{96}\tau^{48\gamma}|\log(1/\tau)|^{96}\longrightarrow0\] for every fixed \(\gamma>0\). A sufficiently small fixed interval length absorbs the error term. The number of intervals depends on \(T\) and the constants, not on \(\tau\); iterating gives \(C_T\tau^\gamma\log(1/\tau)=o(\tau^{\gamma/2})\). This verifies the scalar closure arithmetic. It does not justify a uniform assertion as \(\gamma\downarrow0\), and the theorem does not make one. In particular, this review has not reconstructed every boundary term when restarting the stability estimate at a nonzero time.

