An exact bounded check of the intended harmonic product is available. For \(\psi_0(x)=\pi^{-1/4}e^{-x^2/2}\), \(N\psi_0=\psi_0\). A Gaussian calculation, with square-root branch continuous from \(h=0\), gives \[\left\lVert e^{ihP^2}e^{ihQ^2}\psi_0-e^{ihN}\psi_0\right\rVert^2
 =2-2\operatorname{Re}
       \frac{e^{-ih}}{\sqrt{1-2ih-2h^2}} .\] Its leading norm error is \(\sqrt2\,h^2+O(h^4)\), consistent with an \(O(h^2)\) local error and the stated upper-bound scale. This checks one smooth vector, not all unbounded-domain steps. No published numerical experiment was reproduced.

For a sparsity pattern \(J\), the paper correctly obtains \[MM^*=\operatorname{diag}(d_i),\qquad
 d_i=\sum_{(i,j)\in J}|v_j|^2 .\] It then writes \(f_{\varepsilon,y}=\left\lVert(Av+\varepsilon y)\odot h\right\rVert^2\) with \(h_i=(d_i^2+\varepsilon)^{-1/2}\). The correct weight is \[h_i=(d_i+\varepsilon)^{-1/2}.\] Furthermore, putting \(b=Av+\varepsilon y\) and \(\rho_j=\sum_{(i,j)\in J}|z_i|^2\), the correct gradient is \[\nabla f_{\varepsilon,y}
 =2A^*(h^2\odot b)-2(\rho\odot v),\] where the square is componentwise. The printed first term uses \(h\).

Theorem 1, PDF pp. 2 and 10, concerns a finite totally ordered persistence module \(f:\{0,\ldots,m\}\to R\text{-Mod}\), where \(R\) is a PID and each \(f_a\) is free and finitely generated. It asserts \[f\text{ decomposes into }R\text{-intervals}
 \quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
 \operatorname{coker}f(a\le b)\text{ is free for every }a\le b.\] The interval maps inside their support are identities. The assertion does not extend as stated to arbitrary rings, pointwise torsion modules, or multi-parameter indexing.

For the independent cell and face polynomial unknowns, write \[E_h(v)=\sum_t\left(\|\nabla v_t\|_{L^2(t)}^2+
       \sum_{f\subset\partial t}h_t^{-1}
            \|v_f-v_t\|_{L^2(f)}^2\right),\] and, with \(m_f\) the average of \(w_f\), \[T_h(w)=\sum_{f\subset\partial\Omega}
      h_f^{-1}\|w_f-m_f\|_{L^2(f)}^2
 +\sum_{f\ne g}|f|\,|g|\frac{|m_f-m_g|^2}{|x_f-x_g|^d}.\] The second sum uses ordered pairs. Theorems 2.2–2.3 assert \(T_h(\gamma v)\lesssim E_h(v)\) and a linear right inverse \(L_h\) with \(E_h(L_hw)\lesssim T_h(w)\). Both \(E_h\) and \(T_h\) are squared seminorms.

The sampling step in Lemma 3.2 (p. 7) is also quantitatively consistent. For the selected random weight \(\psi_z\), let \(\mu=\mathbb E\psi_z>8\delta\). The stated variance bound yields \[\mathbb P(\psi_z<\delta)
 \leq \frac{\delta\mu}{(\mu-\delta)^2}
 <\frac{8}{49}<\frac16.\] Together with the two Markov bounds and the large-\(m\) binomial bound, this leaves positive probability for the required selection. The entropy calculation on p. 8 gives a family \(\mathcal I\) of \(\varepsilon m\)-element sets with \(|\mathcal I|<(15\varepsilon)^{-\varepsilon m}\), where \(\varepsilon=\lambda p/5\); rounding is subject to the paper’s large-size convention. Its decisive combination with Lemma 3.1 is \[|\mathcal I|p^{\varepsilon m}
 <\left(\frac{p}{15\varepsilon}\right)^{\varepsilon m}
 =\left(\frac{1}{3\lambda}\right)^{\varepsilon m}
 \leq (2/3)^{\varepsilon m}.\] Thus the family size does not overwhelm the small probability. On the covering event, the surviving stopped contributions force enough \(Q_v^*\) events; the threshold on \(H_v\) supplies the accumulated transition mass required by Lemma 3.2. These are the two separate ingredients needed for Theorem 1.2.

Equation reference: eq:correctkernel. First, detailed balance and Cauchy–Schwarz give \[
 \frac{\varphi^{t+s}(v,w)}{\pi_w}
 \leq
 \left(\frac{\varphi^{2t}(v,v)}{\pi_v}
             \frac{\varphi^{2s}(w,w)}{\pi_w}\right)^{1/2}.\] Lemma 2.1 instead prints \(\pi_v\) under its left-hand numerator. For a simple star with \(N\geq2\) leaves, take \(v\) a leaf, \(w\) the center, \(t=1\), and \(s=2\). Then \(\pi_v=1/(2N)\), \(\pi_w=1/2\), \(\varphi^3(v,w)=1\), \(\varphi^2(v,v)=1/N\), and \(\varphi^4(w,w)=1\). The printed inequality becomes \(2N\leq2\), whereas [eq:correctkernel] (#eq:correctkernel) gives equality \(2\leq2\). All of these chains are finite, connected, simple-graph random walks and reversible; \(N\) can be arbitrarily large.

Equation reference: eq:change. For \(N\) pairs, let \(\mathrm{LP}_N\) denote the noncrossing perfect matchings of \(1,\ldots,2N\). By (1.6), \(\mathcal M_{\alpha,\beta}=1\) precisely when the union of the two matchings forms one meander loop; otherwise it is zero. Equations (1.7) and (4.7) give \[
 \mathcal Z=\mathcal M^{-1}\mathcal F,\qquad \mathcal F_\beta=\sum_{\alpha\in\mathrm{LP}_N}
 \mathcal M_{\beta,\alpha}\mathcal Z_\alpha .\] For an adjacent pair \(j,j+1\), let \(r_j\) remove that pair when it is a link, and let \(t_j\) tie the two vertices, reconnect their former partners, and remove the newly formed pair when it was not a link. Remaining vertices are relabeled increasingly. These are respectively the paper’s \(\widehat\varrho_j\) and \(\widehat\wp_j\). Section 4.2 explicitly distinguishes the bijection \(r_j\) from the generally noninjective surjection \(t_j\) (manuscript p. 42; journal p. 64).

