For \(Q_hp(x)=e^{hx}p(x)/L(h)\), direct integration gives \[L_{Q_hp}(t)=\frac{L(t+h)}{L(h)},\quad
 m_h=K'(h),\quad
 \sigma_h^2=K''(h)=\frac{L(h)L''(h)-L'(h)^2}{L(h)^2}>0.       \tag{2.1}\] Positivity uses nondegeneracy of the tilted distribution. Its support is the same as that of \(X\). Convolution and scaling of this probability tilt give, with \(x,h\in\mathbb R\), \[\frac{p_n(x\sqrt n)}{\varphi(x\sqrt n)}
 =\sqrt{2\pi}\exp\{n(x-h)^2/2-nA(h)\}
     Q_{h\sqrt n}p_n(x\sqrt n).                            \tag{2.2}\] This independently reproduces (3.7), p. 7.

Since \(A''\le1\) and \(A\ge0\), the quadratic upper Taylor bound, minimized over its increment, gives \((A')^2\le2A\). The elementary density–variance bound \(M^2\operatorname{Var}\ge1/12\), combined with the Gaussian envelope, gives \[\sigma_h^{-2}\le(6/\pi)C^2e^{2A(h)}.                       \tag{2.3}\] Consequently the tilted variance stays uniformly away from zero in the critical zones. The tilted third-moment bound of Lemma 5.2 and the stated local limit estimate lead to \[\frac{p_n(x\sqrt n)}{\varphi(x\sqrt n)}
 =\frac{\exp\{-nA(x)-nv_x^2/2\}}{\sigma_x}
       +O(C^4n^{-1/2}),\qquad v_x=\frac{A'(x)}{\sigma_x},   \tag{2.4}\] uniformly when \(A(x)\le a/(n-1)\) and \(n\ge4(a+1)\), as in Lemma 7.1, pp. 14–15. Outside that set, the envelope in (4.3), p. 9, decays as \(C\sqrt2e^{-(n-1)A(x)}\).

Equation reference: eq:fixed. Let \(\mathcal O_D\) be the inclusion-maximal loops contained in the hexagonal domain \(D\), and \(V_D(f)\) the area of its surrounding member, or zero. Their interiors are disjoint. A safe non-optimal pathwise bound is \[
\frac1{|F_D|}\sum_{f\in F_D}\mathbf1_{\{V_D(f)>s\}}
\le\frac{36c}{|F_D|}\sum_{f\in F_D}
L(f)\mathbf1_{\{L(f)>\sqrt{s/c}\}} .\] Indeed, area is at most \(c|\ell|^2\), each loop has at least \(|\ell|/6\) interior bordering faces, and each face borders at most six distinct loops. All such interior faces lie in \(D\), and \(L(f)\ge|\ell|\). Summing gives [eq:fixed] (#eq:fixed). The constant 36 is deliberately generous.

