Equation reference: eq:shift. Equation (5.24), p. 531, applies Lemma 5.6 to two different times but omits \(e^{\mathrm{es}h}\). Define \[U_\xi(t;x,y)=E_x[e^{\int_0^t\xi(X_s)\,ds};X_t\ge y].\] Since \(\zeta=\xi-\mathrm{es}\), the exact identity is \(U_\xi(t;x,y)=e^{\mathrm{es}t}U_\zeta(t;x,y)\). For \(v=(y-x)/t\), \(v'=(y-x)/(t+h)\), (5.23) therefore gives \[
\frac{U_\xi(t+h;x,y)}{U_\xi(t;x,y)}
\le e^{\mathrm{es}h}C_{12}^{\,2}
       \frac{Y^\approx_{v'}(x,y)}{Y^\approx_v(x,y)} .\] The omitted factor cannot be absorbed into a fixed prefactor in (5.24). Indeed the opposite halves of (5.23) give a lower bound with \(e^{\mathrm{es}h}C_{12}^{-2}\) times the same positive ratio. Fix \(v\) in the interior of \(V\), set \(x=0,y=vt\), take \(A>v\), and use \(h=\sqrt t\), \(\delta=1/2\). For large \(t\), all the proposition’s endpoint and speed conditions hold. The printed bound would force \(e^{\mathrm{es}\sqrt t}\le C_{12}^{4}\), a contradiction.

