Equation reference: untouched. Even after multiplicities are fixed, (29) is incorrect with the definition (27). Take \(\xi=\delta_{\{a,b\}}+\delta_{\{z\}}\), where \(a\sim b\) and \(z\) is any vertex. There is exactly one active edge, so the preceding counting problem is absent. The only split replaces \(\{a,b\}\) by \(\{a\},\{b\}\). By additivity across herds, the right-hand side of (29) minus (27) is \[
 F_{v+\varepsilon}(t)-F_v(t).\] It is not zero in general. A direct generator calculation at a singleton gives \[\left.\frac{d^k}{dt^k}
   (F_{v+\varepsilon}(t)-F_v(t))\right|_{t=0}=0
 \quad (k=0,1,2),\qquad
 \left.\frac{d^3}{dt^3}
   (F_{v+\varepsilon}(t)-F_v(t))\right|_{t=0}
       =2d\lambda^2\varepsilon .\] To see the last coefficient, write \(c=d\lambda-1\) and \(L_v^2X=cL_vX-2\lambda L_vH\). A singleton gives an adjacent pair at total rate \(d\lambda\). At that pair, splitting decreases \(H\) by one, so the \(v\)-dependent part of \(L_v^2H\) at the singleton is \(-d\lambda v\). No lower-order term depends on \(v\). This proves the displayed difference.

Submultiplicativity correctly implies \[F_v(s)\geq \frac{F_v(t)}{F_v(t-s)}
 \geq \frac{F_v(t)}{M_v},\qquad
 M_v=\max_{0\leq r\leq1}F_v(r),\quad t-1\leq s\leq t .\] Thus the minimum in the denominator must be replaced by a maximum. If \(q>0\) denotes the probability of the coupling event actually used in the proof, \(\gamma=q/M_v\) is a valid choice. Here \(M_v\) is finite and positive. Joint finite-time continuity, obtained by the same bounded-time coupling and moment domination, makes \(M_v\) continuous on the parameter domain. The prescribed finite exponential clock event has positive continuous probability. The stated positivity and continuous dependence can therefore be retained.

