The principal dependency examined is \[\begin{gathered}
\text{(A1)--(A4) and Green comparison}\ \longrightarrow\ \text{Theorem 1.3},\\
\text{Theorem 1.3}\ \longrightarrow\ \text{Corollary 1.5}
\ \longrightarrow\ \text{Theorem 1.6}.
\end{gathered}\] The later geometric result, Theorem 1.10, additionally assumes positive subordinator drift. Its critical-cone mechanism was examined separately. The appendix estimate behind Theorems 2.8 and 2.9 was also checked.

The required bound follows without separating the maxima: \[\begin{aligned}
 M&\geq\frac1d\sum_{i=1}^d
       \left(\tfrac12 a_{ii}+c_2 B_i\right) \notag\\
  &\geq \frac{\min\{1/2,c_2\}}{d}
     \left(\operatorname{tr}A+\sum_i B_i\right)
   =\frac{\min\{1/2,c_2\}}d\,4r^2K(2r).\end{aligned}\] Inserting this in the preceding Gaussian integral yields \(C/\sqrt{tK(2r)}\), precisely the estimate needed in Case (i). The subsequent use of independent increments then gives its desired \(C/[t\Phi(2r)]\) bound. Thus this is a demonstrated invalid algebraic step, with a complete local repair. It does not refute Lemma 4.1, Pruitt’s exit-time estimate in Theorem 2.8, or the application to (A4) in Theorem 2.9. Its limited impact supports Minor severity. The exact rational example and the case hypothesis were independently checked in `verification/0512/evidence/coordinate_max_check.py`.

Equation reference: eq:gap, eq:boundary. Fix any potential satisfying (A1)–(A2) and \(H_{fp}\), and fix \(d>0\). For large \(N\), \(x_N=L_N-d>1\). The explicit tail (43) gives \[v_L(L-d)=\frac{\sinh(\beta_Ld)}
                   {\sinh(\beta_L(L-1))},
 \qquad \beta_L=\sqrt{2\lambda_1(L)}.\] The valid eigenvalue-gap calculation (45) implies \((\beta-\beta_L)L\to0\). One can independently obtain its leading term from the endpoint derivative formula \[\lambda_1'(L)=\frac{v_L'(L)^2}{2\|v_L\|_2^2}
 \sim\frac{2\beta^2e^{2\beta}}{I}e^{-2\beta L},\] then integrate from \(L\) to infinity. Hence \[
 w_L\sim \frac{\beta e^{2\beta}}{I}e^{-2\beta L}
       =\frac{\beta e^{2\beta}}{I}N^{-(\alpha-1)}.\] Together with \(\|v_L\|_2^2\to I\), the explicit tail yields \[
 \frac{H_L(L-d)}{N}
 \longrightarrow
 K_d:=\frac{2e^\beta}{\widetilde c I}
          e^{-\mu d}\sinh(\beta d)>0 .\] This is a limit of the precise normalized weight in (20); there is no freedom to remove the factor \(N\).

Equation reference: eq:wronskian. There is an independent exact identity for the Wronskian. Let \(v=v_L\) and \(F(y)=\psi_\lambda(y)v'(y)-\psi_\lambda'(y)v(y)\). Then \[F'(y)=2(\lambda_1-\lambda)\psi_\lambda(y)v(y),\quad
 F(L)=0,\quad F(0)=\omega_\lambda,\] where the last equality uses \(\varphi_\lambda'(0)=v_L'(0)\). Consequently \[
 \omega_{\lambda_\infty+\xi}
   =2(\xi+w_L)\int_0^L\psi_{\lambda_\infty+\xi}(y)v_L(y)\,dy .\] Under \(H_{fp}\) and \(N^{-1}\lesssim\xi\ll L^{-1}\), the elementary continuous-dependence estimates of Lemma 9 give \[\omega_{\lambda_\infty+\xi}
 =2(\xi+w_L)\bigl(\|v_L\|_2^2+O(\xi L)\bigr)
 \sim 2\xi I .\] Lemma 10 instead states leading term \(\xi I\). Retaining \(\xi+w_L\) also avoids suppressing a relative error \(w_L/\xi\) that need not be \(O(\xi L)\). For example, \(\xi=N^{-1}\) and \(2<\alpha<3\) give \(w_L/\xi\asymp N^{-(\alpha-2)}\), larger than \(L/N\). ◻

